1
00:02:16,177 --> 00:02:17,637
Relax.

2
00:02:18,095 --> 00:02:19,930
Anything in here
would be dead by now.

3
00:02:40,993 --> 00:02:42,995
There she is,

4
00:02:43,704 --> 00:02:45,956
the Indominus rex.

5
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:28,791
Specimen
collected. Sent to the surface.

6
00:03:28,999 --> 00:03:30,000
Roger that.

7
00:03:30,167 --> 00:03:32,420
Air One, clear for takeoff.
Begin tracking.

8
00:03:32,586 --> 00:03:33,754
Copy that.
Go, go!

9
00:03:33,921 --> 00:03:35,047
Tracking on.

10
00:03:46,559 --> 00:03:49,645
Land One, the asset
is secure. We got what we came for.

11
00:03:49,854 --> 00:03:51,647
We're coming back for you.
Close the doors.

12
00:03:52,273 --> 00:03:54,567
Marine One, I gotta close
the gates. Get out of there.

13
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,986
Understood.
We're heading out.

14
00:04:17,214 --> 00:04:19,133
Marine One, I need to close
the gates. Confirm position.

15
00:04:19,633 --> 00:04:20,843
Can you hear me?
Are you out?

16
00:04:21,051 --> 00:04:22,344
Marine One?

17
00:04:24,013 --> 00:04:25,890
Guys?
What is that?

18
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:31,812
Marine One,
I'm closing the gates.

19
00:04:36,984 --> 00:04:38,986
Seriously, guys, I'm kind
of exposed out here.

20
00:04:39,153 --> 00:04:42,448
Marine One. Confirm
you're out, Marine One.

21
00:04:42,531 --> 00:04:44,950
Confirm position. Can you hear
me, Marine One, where are you?

22
00:04:47,286 --> 00:04:48,579
Come on!

23
00:04:49,747 --> 00:04:51,457
I can't reach them!

24
00:04:51,624 --> 00:04:53,167
Come on!
What?

25
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:56,921
Behind you!

26
00:04:57,087 --> 00:05:00,257
What is going on?
I can't hear you!

27
00:05:13,145 --> 00:05:15,564
No, no! Wait!
Don't go! No!

28
00:05:19,109 --> 00:05:20,110
Throw him the ladder.

29
00:05:20,194 --> 00:05:21,737
Come on!
Bring it down!

30
00:05:23,405 --> 00:05:24,615
Slow down! No, no, no!

31
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:27,535
Don't go!

32
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:33,791
Oh, God!

33
00:05:36,293 --> 00:05:38,546
No, wait! No, no! No!

34
00:05:45,678 --> 00:05:46,720
Oh, God, no!

35
00:05:48,514 --> 00:05:49,515
God, no!

36
00:05:52,268 --> 00:05:53,602
We're gonna stall!

37
00:05:53,769 --> 00:05:54,895
Cut it now!

38
00:05:55,062 --> 00:05:56,981
- Give him a chance!
- We're gonna die here!

39
00:05:57,147 --> 00:05:58,399
Come on! Come on!

40
00:05:58,566 --> 00:06:00,025
No, no, no!

41
00:06:00,192 --> 00:06:01,527
Oh, God!

42
00:06:02,945 --> 00:06:03,946
I said cut it!

43
00:06:04,113 --> 00:06:06,323
Don't do it! It's horrible!

44
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:08,117
I don't wanna die!

45
00:06:11,287 --> 00:06:13,289
Yeah!

46
00:06:17,209 --> 00:06:19,211
Yeah!
Come on!

47
00:06:41,525 --> 00:06:44,820
DNA sample secured. Tell
the boss we're coming back home.

48
00:07:14,850 --> 00:07:17,144
Three years after
the fall of Jurassic World,

49
00:07:17,227 --> 00:07:19,730
the debate
over Isla Nublar rages on.

50
00:07:20,397 --> 00:07:23,943
The island's long-dormant
volcano, reclassified as active,

51
00:07:24,151 --> 00:07:26,904
has shown considerable unrest
in recent months.

52
00:07:27,446 --> 00:07:29,949
Geologists now predict
an extinction-level event

53
00:07:30,157 --> 00:07:33,285
will kill off the last living
dinosaurs on the planet.

54
00:07:33,702 --> 00:07:36,497
Since the disaster that
shocked the world in 2015,

55
00:07:36,705 --> 00:07:40,292
the Masrani Corporation has paid out
more than 800 million in damages

56
00:07:40,501 --> 00:07:43,504
to settle class-action
lawsuits brought by survivors.

57
00:07:43,921 --> 00:07:46,465
Activist groups have mobilized
around the globe

58
00:07:46,715 --> 00:07:50,427
in what has become the flashpoint
animal rights issue of our time.

59
00:07:51,011 --> 00:07:53,222
With an eruption expected
at any moment,

60
00:07:53,472 --> 00:07:56,016
the US Senate has convened
a special committee

61
00:07:56,183 --> 00:07:58,060
to answer a grave,
moral question.

62
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:02,898
Do dinosaurs deserve the same protections
given to other endangered species

63
00:08:03,065 --> 00:08:05,234
or should they be left to die?

64
00:08:05,943 --> 00:08:09,488
I think that we should allow

65
00:08:10,364 --> 00:08:12,366
our magnificent
and glorious dinosaurs

66
00:08:13,659 --> 00:08:15,202
to be taken out by the volcano.

67
00:08:16,537 --> 00:08:17,997
Murderer.

68
00:08:18,455 --> 00:08:19,623
Silence, please.

69
00:08:19,748 --> 00:08:21,625
As deeply sad as that would be,

70
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:25,129
we altered the course
of natural history.

71
00:08:26,755 --> 00:08:27,798
This is a correction.

72
00:08:27,923 --> 00:08:32,011
Are you suggesting the Almighty is
taking matters in his own hands?

73
00:08:32,094 --> 00:08:35,931
Senator, with all due respect, God's
not part of the equation, no.

74
00:08:36,015 --> 00:08:38,142
What I mean is that,
in the last century,

75
00:08:38,225 --> 00:08:41,812
we amassed landmark
technological power

76
00:08:42,312 --> 00:08:46,066
and we've consistently proven ourselves
incapable of handling that power.

77
00:08:46,734 --> 00:08:49,528
Eighty years ago, who could've
predicted nuclear proliferation?

78
00:08:49,653 --> 00:08:51,739
But then there it was. And
now we've got genetic power,

79
00:08:51,905 --> 00:08:55,284
so how long is it gonna take for
that to spread around the globe

80
00:08:55,451 --> 00:08:57,578
and what's gonna be
done with it?

81
00:08:58,954 --> 00:09:01,165
It ain't gonna stop with the
de-extinction of the dinosaurs.

82
00:09:02,416 --> 00:09:04,126
I'm not sure I know
what you're talking about.

83
00:09:04,209 --> 00:09:07,796
I'm talking about man-made,

84
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:10,841
cataclysmic change.

85
00:09:11,759 --> 00:09:12,801
What kind of change?

86
00:09:14,053 --> 00:09:15,637
Change is like death.

87
00:09:17,097 --> 00:09:20,392
You don't know what it looks like
until you're standing at the gates.

88
00:09:36,450 --> 00:09:37,618
Yes, absolutely!

89
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:41,955
We would love to have a
photo op with the senator.

90
00:09:42,122 --> 00:09:44,750
As a matter of fact, I do have the
dinosaurs' best interests in mind.

91
00:09:44,917 --> 00:09:46,001
I'm a paleo-veterinarian.

92
00:09:46,168 --> 00:09:47,336
Is that a real thing?

93
00:09:47,503 --> 00:09:48,545
That's a real thing.

94
00:09:48,962 --> 00:09:50,547
No, I haven't seen one
with my own eyes.

95
00:09:50,631 --> 00:09:52,883
You know, not everyone can afford
to spend their life savings...

96
00:09:53,467 --> 00:09:54,510
Claire!

97
00:09:55,719 --> 00:09:57,012
Oh, uh...

98
00:09:57,096 --> 00:09:58,514
Hello. Hello, um...

99
00:09:58,597 --> 00:10:00,224
Congresswoman Delgado.

100
00:10:00,432 --> 00:10:01,475
Congresswoman Delgado.

101
00:10:01,642 --> 00:10:03,477
I'm Claire. I'm the lead
organizer here.

102
00:10:04,311 --> 00:10:06,230
Of course, but if I could just
get a moment of your time?

103
00:10:08,107 --> 00:10:10,192
Great. Thank you. Um...

104
00:10:10,359 --> 00:10:12,945
So, our mission here at the
Dinosaur Protection Group

105
00:10:13,112 --> 00:10:16,073
is to secure federal funding
for a safe, natural...

106
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:17,825
That was, like, 40 seconds.
You're getting better.

107
00:10:18,242 --> 00:10:19,827
You gotta stop letting
your mom shop for you.

108
00:10:19,993 --> 00:10:22,788
You don't need to insult me every
time someone pisses you off.

109
00:10:22,996 --> 00:10:25,207
Dude, I'm a doctor,
not a telemarketer.

110
00:10:25,374 --> 00:10:26,708
What is your job again?

111
00:10:26,917 --> 00:10:28,210
You plug in cords, right?

112
00:10:28,377 --> 00:10:30,129
It's actually more important
than it sounds.

113
00:10:30,712 --> 00:10:32,548
- Can I ask you a question?
- Go on.

114
00:10:32,714 --> 00:10:34,299
Do you have kids?
Two girls.

115
00:10:34,466 --> 00:10:36,009
Okay. So, your kids,

116
00:10:36,176 --> 00:10:38,303
an entire generation,
has grown up in a world

117
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:40,931
where dinosaurs
are living and breathing.

118
00:10:41,515 --> 00:10:43,934
But soon, they're gonna have
to watch them go extinct.

119
00:10:44,393 --> 00:10:45,477
Or not.

120
00:10:45,811 --> 00:10:48,063
If people like you
make a difference.

121
00:10:48,856 --> 00:10:51,108
Set up a meeting with my
office after the hearing.

122
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:52,484
Great.

123
00:10:53,652 --> 00:10:54,653
Yes!

124
00:10:55,863 --> 00:10:57,489
We now go live to the Capitol...

125
00:10:57,656 --> 00:11:00,284
- Claire, it's on.
- Turn it up.

126
00:11:00,492 --> 00:11:02,244
...the US government
will take preventative action

127
00:11:02,327 --> 00:11:04,872
to protect the dinosaurs
on Isla Nublar.

128
00:11:05,038 --> 00:11:06,748
After thorough deliberations,

129
00:11:06,832 --> 00:11:10,460
the committee has resolved not to
recommend any legislative action

130
00:11:10,627 --> 00:11:14,006
regarding the de-extinct
creatures on Isla Nublar.

131
00:11:14,506 --> 00:11:16,133
This is an act of God.

132
00:11:16,466 --> 00:11:19,511
And while, of course, we feel
great sympathy for these animals,

133
00:11:19,678 --> 00:11:21,972
we cannot condone
government involvement

134
00:11:22,139 --> 00:11:25,225
on what amounts to
a privately-owned venture.

135
00:11:29,396 --> 00:11:31,773
They're all gonna die
and no one cares.

136
00:11:33,442 --> 00:11:34,443
We do.

137
00:11:39,656 --> 00:11:42,075
Give me one second.
Let's get back to work.

138
00:11:42,534 --> 00:11:44,077
Okay, everyone, come on.

139
00:11:47,998 --> 00:11:49,041
Hello?

140
00:11:49,124 --> 00:11:50,459
May I speak with Ms. Dearing?

141
00:11:50,751 --> 00:11:52,002
Yes, this is Claire Dearing.

142
00:11:52,085 --> 00:11:54,713
Good morning, I'm calling from Mr.
Benjamin Lockwood's estate.

143
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:56,715
Would you mind holding
for a second?

144
00:11:56,882 --> 00:11:58,967
Yes, of course I'll hold.

145
00:12:26,703 --> 00:12:28,080
Ms. Dearing,
you're early.

146
00:12:28,997 --> 00:12:30,123
Would you like to step in?

147
00:12:30,290 --> 00:12:31,541
Thank you.

148
00:12:32,834 --> 00:12:35,504
I will inform Mr. Mills
that you've arrived.

149
00:12:58,527 --> 00:13:01,655
John Alfred Hammond,
the father of Jurassic Park.

150
00:13:01,822 --> 00:13:03,323
But of course you knew that.

151
00:13:03,490 --> 00:13:07,077
Hello, Claire. I'm Eli Mills.
I work for Ben Lockwood.

152
00:13:07,327 --> 00:13:09,246
We actually met once,
seven, eight years ago,

153
00:13:09,329 --> 00:13:11,498
and you don't remember.
Of course I remember.

154
00:13:11,623 --> 00:13:13,667
Haven't you run his foundation since.
. . Since college, yeah.

155
00:13:13,750 --> 00:13:15,294
You remember?
Yes.

156
00:13:15,502 --> 00:13:16,837
Wow. Cool.

157
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:19,965
He wanted someone young and
idealistic to spend his fortune

158
00:13:21,174 --> 00:13:22,384
and I used to be both.

159
00:13:23,969 --> 00:13:25,178
Follow me.

160
00:13:26,722 --> 00:13:28,640
Let me give you
a bit of history.

161
00:13:28,974 --> 00:13:30,225
This is where it all began.

162
00:13:30,392 --> 00:13:33,478
Before the island, Sorna,
Jurassic Park, all of it.

163
00:13:33,645 --> 00:13:37,024
Hammond and Lockwood built a
custom lab in the sub-basement,

164
00:13:37,190 --> 00:13:40,902
extracted the first DNA from
amber right beneath our feet.

165
00:13:41,069 --> 00:13:42,779
Oh, my goodness.

166
00:13:43,238 --> 00:13:45,032
What they did here
was a miracle.

167
00:13:45,991 --> 00:13:47,284
I still believe that.

168
00:13:47,451 --> 00:13:48,952
So do I, Claire.

169
00:13:49,369 --> 00:13:50,370
So do I.

170
00:13:50,454 --> 00:13:53,790
And I still believe it matters
what happens to those animals.

171
00:13:54,249 --> 00:13:58,170
There is something Mr. Lockwood would
very much like you to do, Claire.

172
00:13:58,337 --> 00:14:00,839
You can call it a favor
for an old friend.

173
00:14:01,006 --> 00:14:04,343
We have a piece of land.

174
00:14:04,509 --> 00:14:06,887
A sanctuary protected
by natural barriers,

175
00:14:07,512 --> 00:14:08,889
fully self-sustaining.

176
00:14:09,056 --> 00:14:12,809
A new home where the dinosaurs
will be secure and free.

177
00:14:12,976 --> 00:14:14,061
You're gonna get them out?

178
00:14:14,603 --> 00:14:15,771
You're gonna save them?

179
00:14:15,937 --> 00:14:17,481
No, Ms. Dearing.

180
00:14:17,939 --> 00:14:19,524
I'm going to save us.

181
00:14:20,317 --> 00:14:22,861
We could both use a touch
of redemption, couldn't we?

182
00:14:23,945 --> 00:14:25,113
Lovely to see you again.

183
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:26,698
And you, Mr. Lockwood.

184
00:14:28,492 --> 00:14:30,994
This was all
John Hammond's dream.

185
00:14:31,161 --> 00:14:33,789
To let these creatures
live in peace.

186
00:14:33,955 --> 00:14:37,584
So, we have created sanctuary.

187
00:14:37,793 --> 00:14:39,336
No fences. No cages.

188
00:14:40,087 --> 00:14:41,463
No tourists.

189
00:14:41,755 --> 00:14:43,840
Just as Mother Nature intended.

190
00:14:44,383 --> 00:14:45,717
John said it best.

191
00:14:47,010 --> 00:14:49,513
"These creatures
don't need our protection.

192
00:14:50,222 --> 00:14:52,557
"They need our absence."

193
00:14:55,352 --> 00:14:58,438
When we were young,
we both shared this passion,

194
00:14:58,688 --> 00:15:00,023
John and I.

195
00:15:01,108 --> 00:15:02,651
What fools we were,

196
00:15:04,361 --> 00:15:07,614
trying to run before
we'd learned to walk.

197
00:15:07,781 --> 00:15:09,991
As all young men do, I suppose.

198
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:13,954
But we learned.

199
00:15:14,454 --> 00:15:16,206
Unfortunately, in the end,

200
00:15:16,915 --> 00:15:18,417
it drove us apart.

201
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:25,006
Life teaches us
some very hard lessons,

202
00:15:25,173 --> 00:15:26,299
doesn't it, Claire?

203
00:15:28,385 --> 00:15:29,511
Yes.

204
00:15:29,719 --> 00:15:31,596
Sir Benjamin.
Hmm?

205
00:15:31,930 --> 00:15:32,973
Ah.

206
00:15:33,056 --> 00:15:34,391
My bloody medicines.

207
00:15:35,183 --> 00:15:36,768
Will you excuse me, Claire?

208
00:15:36,852 --> 00:15:37,853
Of course.

209
00:15:38,145 --> 00:15:40,564
Eli here will
help you with the details.

210
00:15:41,606 --> 00:15:43,233
We will save them.

211
00:15:44,943 --> 00:15:46,319
What a gift

212
00:15:48,530 --> 00:15:49,531
for our children.

213
00:15:50,449 --> 00:15:51,450
Thank you.

214
00:15:52,951 --> 00:15:54,161
Iris?

215
00:15:58,748 --> 00:16:01,293
Does he have children? I thought I saw.
. . Was there a little girl?

216
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:02,752
He has a grandchild.

217
00:16:02,919 --> 00:16:05,464
His daughter died
in a car accident.

218
00:16:05,839 --> 00:16:06,840
That's awful.

219
00:16:06,923 --> 00:16:09,676
Yeah, I know, but they're very close.
Really close.

220
00:16:11,303 --> 00:16:13,013
So, what do you need from me?

221
00:16:13,430 --> 00:16:15,974
There was a tracking
system in place at your park.

222
00:16:16,141 --> 00:16:18,101
Radio-frequency ID chips
in each dinosaur.

223
00:16:18,310 --> 00:16:19,311
I remember.
Right.

224
00:16:19,478 --> 00:16:21,104
So, if we could
access that system,

225
00:16:21,271 --> 00:16:24,024
our ability to locate and
capture those animals safely

226
00:16:24,483 --> 00:16:25,567
would increase tenfold.

227
00:16:25,734 --> 00:16:28,153
We need your handprint
to access the system,

228
00:16:28,487 --> 00:16:30,113
but what I really need, Claire,

229
00:16:31,823 --> 00:16:32,824
is you.

230
00:16:33,325 --> 00:16:35,660
Moving endangered species
isn't exactly legal,

231
00:16:35,827 --> 00:16:37,287
but it's the right thing to do.

232
00:16:37,496 --> 00:16:38,955
No one knows this park
as well as you do

233
00:16:39,039 --> 00:16:40,332
and we need that expertise.

234
00:16:42,042 --> 00:16:44,044
How many can you save?
Eleven species. For sure.

235
00:16:44,169 --> 00:16:47,380
I mean, more if we can,
but time is against us.

236
00:16:47,506 --> 00:16:51,218
I'm afraid there is
one animal in particular

237
00:16:51,801 --> 00:16:53,762
that poses a real challenge
for us.

238
00:16:55,305 --> 00:16:56,598
Blue.
I didn't know she had a name,

239
00:16:56,765 --> 00:16:58,350
but Blue is potentially

240
00:16:58,433 --> 00:17:00,727
the second-most intelligent
piece of life on this planet

241
00:17:00,894 --> 00:17:03,438
and she's the last of her kind.
She must be preserved.

242
00:17:03,605 --> 00:17:06,691
She can pick up your scent a mile off.
You'll never capture her.

243
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:11,738
Well, we thought you might
know someone who could help.

244
00:17:12,781 --> 00:17:14,866
Maybe you could convince him.

245
00:17:35,679 --> 00:17:40,267
Oh, Time, she
is a freight train chugging

246
00:17:41,560 --> 00:17:44,813
Oh, stalling
Time is, too

247
00:17:45,814 --> 00:17:48,817
When the light
at the end of the tunnel

248
00:17:51,570 --> 00:17:55,115
Is growing small behind you

249
00:17:55,198 --> 00:17:56,199
Hey, Owen.

250
00:17:57,409 --> 00:18:00,620
I still love you in the morning

251
00:18:00,704 --> 00:18:01,705
Owen!

252
00:18:02,914 --> 00:18:04,082
Oh, boy.

253
00:18:11,381 --> 00:18:12,465
Hi.

254
00:18:13,883 --> 00:18:15,343
Back for more, huh?

255
00:18:16,136 --> 00:18:17,762
Can I buy you a beer?

256
00:18:18,597 --> 00:18:20,932
Did you bring them, or do we
gotta, like, go somewhere?

257
00:18:28,773 --> 00:18:30,150
I'm sorry.

258
00:18:30,317 --> 00:18:31,401
Seriously?

259
00:18:31,568 --> 00:18:33,486
I can't believe you think
that you left me!

260
00:18:33,737 --> 00:18:35,614
Replay the conversation
in your head, all right?

261
00:18:35,739 --> 00:18:38,491
You said, "You wanna go live
in your van, like a bum?

262
00:18:38,742 --> 00:18:40,243
"Go ahead, Owen."

263
00:18:40,410 --> 00:18:41,828
And I said, "Okay."

264
00:18:42,162 --> 00:18:43,330
So, how is that you leaving me?

265
00:18:43,496 --> 00:18:45,332
Because I left. I left you.

266
00:18:45,498 --> 00:18:48,043
You left because I told you to.

267
00:18:48,251 --> 00:18:49,919
And then, I left.

268
00:18:50,378 --> 00:18:52,464
Because you didn't wanna live in
a van on the side of the road.

269
00:18:52,631 --> 00:18:53,882
No. No, no, no.
Remember?

270
00:18:53,965 --> 00:18:56,760
Because you wouldn't let me drive
the damn van for five minutes.

271
00:18:56,926 --> 00:18:58,428
I'm chivalrous.
What can I do?

272
00:18:58,595 --> 00:18:59,929
You are so stubborn.

273
00:19:00,138 --> 00:19:02,057
Well, look at you now.
You're saving the world.

274
00:19:02,223 --> 00:19:03,266
I'm trying, at least.

275
00:19:03,350 --> 00:19:05,435
You can't just run away
from everything, Owen.

276
00:19:05,602 --> 00:19:06,728
So, you're what...

277
00:19:06,978 --> 00:19:08,355
Dating an accountant now?

278
00:19:09,105 --> 00:19:10,523
An insurance actuary?

279
00:19:11,650 --> 00:19:12,776
Your skin looks nice.

280
00:19:15,528 --> 00:19:16,529
Dermatologist?

281
00:19:16,905 --> 00:19:18,031
Owen...

282
00:19:18,114 --> 00:19:19,449
Does he check you for moles?
Stop.

283
00:19:19,616 --> 00:19:20,617
A ventriloquist?

284
00:19:20,784 --> 00:19:22,285
This is not
why we're here, okay?

285
00:19:22,494 --> 00:19:23,953
Yeah, I know why we're here.

286
00:19:24,829 --> 00:19:26,373
Lockwood's little
flunky called me.

287
00:19:27,207 --> 00:19:28,249
Rescue op.

288
00:19:28,375 --> 00:19:31,211
Save the dinosaurs from an
island that's about to explode.

289
00:19:31,419 --> 00:19:33,088
What could go wrong?
I'm going.

290
00:19:33,254 --> 00:19:34,964
Don't.
I don't have a choice.

291
00:19:35,131 --> 00:19:36,424
What? Of course
you have a choice.

292
00:19:36,591 --> 00:19:38,718
So, what, I should just
build my own cabin,

293
00:19:38,802 --> 00:19:40,512
play pool, and drink beer
all day

294
00:19:40,595 --> 00:19:42,514
while these dinosaurs
go extinct?

295
00:19:42,681 --> 00:19:44,683
Yeah. I like pool.

296
00:19:45,809 --> 00:19:46,935
Blue is alive.

297
00:19:48,895 --> 00:19:49,896
Jesus, Claire!

298
00:19:50,021 --> 00:19:51,356
You raised her, Owen.

299
00:19:51,856 --> 00:19:54,192
You spent years of your life
working with her.

300
00:19:54,943 --> 00:19:56,695
You're just gonna let her die?

301
00:19:58,780 --> 00:19:59,781
Well, yeah.

302
00:20:03,326 --> 00:20:06,371
Come on, you're a better man
than you think you are.

303
00:20:08,331 --> 00:20:10,291
You should write
fortune cookies.

304
00:20:12,460 --> 00:20:13,670
Forget it...

305
00:20:14,170 --> 00:20:16,214
There's a charter flight
leaving tomorrow morning.

306
00:20:16,673 --> 00:20:19,050
You're on the manifest.
I just wanted to let you know.

307
00:20:35,567 --> 00:20:37,610
All right, chow time, chow time.

308
00:20:38,153 --> 00:20:39,320
Whoa, whoa.

309
00:20:39,446 --> 00:20:40,905
Back up. Back off.

310
00:20:41,072 --> 00:20:43,241
Hey, hey, hey. Jeez.

311
00:20:43,450 --> 00:20:45,702
Take it easy. Echo.

312
00:20:46,619 --> 00:20:48,621
Jeez, there's plenty of food.

313
00:20:49,664 --> 00:20:51,499
God, you guys been
eating sugar or something?

314
00:20:51,666 --> 00:20:52,751
You're hyper today.

315
00:20:54,753 --> 00:20:57,172
Okay, okay. Back up.

316
00:20:57,255 --> 00:20:59,090
Thank you, Blue.

317
00:21:01,176 --> 00:21:02,510
Whoa, whoa.

318
00:21:07,891 --> 00:21:08,892
Did you get that?

319
00:21:18,109 --> 00:21:21,654
Relax. You're more likely to die
riding on a horse than in a plane.

320
00:21:21,821 --> 00:21:23,656
No, I'm not, because
I won't get on a horse.

321
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:24,741
My chances are zero.

322
00:21:24,866 --> 00:21:26,201
Planes are
the safest way to travel.

323
00:21:26,367 --> 00:21:29,370
You call this a plane? My
cousin has a drone bigger than this.

324
00:21:36,795 --> 00:21:38,630
Owen!

325
00:21:38,963 --> 00:21:40,715
Franklin, you wiener,
get on the plane.

326
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:42,425
Didn't think you were
gonna ever show up.

327
00:21:43,009 --> 00:21:44,052
Oh, um...

328
00:21:44,135 --> 00:21:45,678
This is Zia Rodriguez.

329
00:21:45,762 --> 00:21:46,638
Zia.

330
00:21:46,721 --> 00:21:48,264
Hey, how you doing? Yeah, real good.
How are you?

331
00:21:48,348 --> 00:21:49,349
Good.

332
00:21:49,432 --> 00:21:51,601
Franklin Webb.
Systems analyst.

333
00:21:53,895 --> 00:21:55,313
Nervous flyer?

334
00:21:55,522 --> 00:21:57,565
Would you ride a thousand-pound horse
that's been abused all its life?

335
00:21:57,732 --> 00:22:00,193
I rode my motorcycle through the
jungle with a pack of raptors.

336
00:22:01,694 --> 00:22:03,613
We're not compatible.

337
00:22:16,668 --> 00:22:17,794
Maisie?

338
00:22:19,671 --> 00:22:22,882
You're always hiding from me. This
is not my job, looking for you.

339
00:22:24,634 --> 00:22:25,635
Maisie!

340
00:22:28,179 --> 00:22:29,556
Maisie!

341
00:22:32,225 --> 00:22:33,476
Come out!

342
00:22:46,990 --> 00:22:48,283
Boo!

343
00:22:48,366 --> 00:22:49,617
You silly sausage.

344
00:22:49,826 --> 00:22:50,827
You'll be the death of me.

345
00:22:50,952 --> 00:22:53,162
You know, one day,
my heart might really stop.

346
00:22:53,621 --> 00:22:54,831
Then what would you do?

347
00:22:55,081 --> 00:22:56,624
Go and live in the forest
with the lions?

348
00:22:56,708 --> 00:22:58,001
There are no lions
in the forest.

349
00:22:58,084 --> 00:22:59,294
Your grandfather's
been asking for you.

350
00:22:59,460 --> 00:23:00,461
Really?
Mmm.

351
00:23:00,545 --> 00:23:02,714
So, you go see your grandfather, and
then you go straight to your bath.

352
00:23:02,881 --> 00:23:04,632
I don't want a bath.
What?

353
00:23:04,841 --> 00:23:05,925
Queen's English, girl.

354
00:23:06,009 --> 00:23:07,176
Bath.
Bath.

355
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:08,094
Bath.
Bath.

356
00:23:08,219 --> 00:23:10,430
Bath, not "bath,"
you're not a wild animal.

357
00:23:10,513 --> 00:23:12,599
Stop.

358
00:23:17,770 --> 00:23:18,771
Hi, Grandpa.

359
00:23:18,855 --> 00:23:20,356
Ah, there you are.

360
00:23:20,815 --> 00:23:22,400
Come over and sit by me.

361
00:23:22,567 --> 00:23:23,651
I've missed you.

362
00:23:23,735 --> 00:23:25,028
I was on safari

363
00:23:25,194 --> 00:23:27,322
all the way through the Cretaceous
to the Jurassic in one day.

364
00:23:27,614 --> 00:23:28,823
Oh, my. What'd you see?

365
00:23:28,907 --> 00:23:31,993
Mostly herbivores.
There was one T. rex, though.

366
00:23:32,493 --> 00:23:35,788
Casualties, of course.
Iris included.

367
00:23:35,914 --> 00:23:37,540
She jumped out of her skin.

368
00:23:38,124 --> 00:23:40,168
You have your
mother's wicked sense of humor.

369
00:23:44,088 --> 00:23:45,840
Do I look like her?

370
00:23:47,133 --> 00:23:48,259
Oh, yes.

371
00:23:49,636 --> 00:23:51,888
You could be her mirror image.

372
00:23:53,222 --> 00:23:55,683
Did my mother
ever visit the park?

373
00:23:57,018 --> 00:23:59,354
Once, a long time ago.

374
00:24:00,688 --> 00:24:02,690
She would've saved them,
too, you know.

375
00:24:03,983 --> 00:24:06,110
She would have saved them all.

376
00:24:58,079 --> 00:25:00,540
That's two klicks past the LZ.

377
00:25:03,376 --> 00:25:04,377
Claire.

378
00:25:08,172 --> 00:25:10,008
Ken Wheatley. Welcome back.

379
00:25:10,133 --> 00:25:12,051
Quite an operation
you've got going on here.

380
00:25:12,135 --> 00:25:15,179
Mr. Lockwood takes his humanitarian
efforts very seriously.

381
00:25:15,388 --> 00:25:17,682
Where's the, uh,
raptor wrangler?

382
00:25:18,725 --> 00:25:19,726
Animal behaviorist.

383
00:25:20,351 --> 00:25:22,645
Owen Grady.
Hey, Owen. Ken Wheatley.

384
00:25:22,812 --> 00:25:24,939
And you are
the Great White Hunter?

385
00:25:26,441 --> 00:25:28,985
I guess. I'm the
expedition facilitator.

386
00:25:29,527 --> 00:25:31,404
Oh, God! It's hot.

387
00:25:33,740 --> 00:25:35,533
It's about to get
a whole lot hotter.

388
00:25:40,621 --> 00:25:43,249
Keep it moving!
You're all clear!

389
00:25:43,833 --> 00:25:46,419
Go! Go! You've got
the green light!

390
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:51,382
This way! Let's go! Let's go!
Keep it moving!

391
00:25:51,883 --> 00:25:53,885
Hold it! Hold up!

392
00:26:03,603 --> 00:26:05,063
Need some bug spray?

393
00:26:05,354 --> 00:26:06,606
Bug spray?

394
00:26:09,233 --> 00:26:11,027
The T. rex would be dead
by now, right?

395
00:26:11,194 --> 00:26:13,321
No, it's impossible to know
the max lifespan of a clone

396
00:26:13,488 --> 00:26:15,531
in a completely different
environment.

397
00:26:15,907 --> 00:26:17,825
Take a caveman that
would've lived 20 years.

398
00:26:17,992 --> 00:26:19,952
Feed him prime meals,
give him health care,

399
00:26:20,119 --> 00:26:22,288
he's gonna live
five times as long.

400
00:26:24,499 --> 00:26:28,544
So, she'd be dead
by now? Right?

401
00:27:14,632 --> 00:27:15,800
Bad memories?

402
00:27:18,177 --> 00:27:19,679
Some were good.

403
00:27:30,815 --> 00:27:32,275
What was that?

404
00:27:40,032 --> 00:27:41,033
Is it the T. rex?

405
00:27:48,916 --> 00:27:50,376
I have to see this.

406
00:27:51,294 --> 00:27:54,046
Hey! Miss? Miss!

407
00:27:55,006 --> 00:27:56,841
This area is not secured.

408
00:28:28,122 --> 00:28:29,707
Look at that.

409
00:28:30,249 --> 00:28:32,585
Never thought
I'd see one in real life.

410
00:28:34,921 --> 00:28:36,505
She's beautiful.

411
00:28:48,184 --> 00:28:49,769
Can we...

412
00:29:03,115 --> 00:29:04,700
Okay, let's go.

413
00:29:09,330 --> 00:29:10,498
Okay. Okay.

414
00:29:10,915 --> 00:29:12,500
Okay, main system...

415
00:29:14,126 --> 00:29:15,127
And turn on the controls.

416
00:29:16,629 --> 00:29:18,089
It should be working.

417
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:28,224
Come on, tiger. Do you
know what you're doing?

418
00:29:33,354 --> 00:29:34,438
After you, tiger.

419
00:29:38,651 --> 00:29:41,237
Are we sure these biometric
chips are still transmitting?

420
00:29:41,320 --> 00:29:42,905
Battery would be dead by now.

421
00:29:43,072 --> 00:29:44,448
The dinosaur is the battery.

422
00:29:44,532 --> 00:29:45,783
They're powered by
body heat and movement.

423
00:29:46,742 --> 00:29:48,202
Hand.

424
00:29:52,164 --> 00:29:53,165
How much longer until...

425
00:29:56,335 --> 00:29:57,461
I'm in.

426
00:30:01,173 --> 00:30:02,800
There's a large concentration
on the east dock.

427
00:30:03,009 --> 00:30:05,469
That's our boat. We've already
captured a bunch of them.

428
00:30:05,636 --> 00:30:07,096
Without the tracking system?

429
00:30:07,555 --> 00:30:09,348
The big herbivores
are easy to find.

430
00:30:09,515 --> 00:30:10,975
Can you isolate that raptor?

431
00:30:11,142 --> 00:30:12,351
I need a species code.
D-9.

432
00:30:16,397 --> 00:30:17,606
There she is.

433
00:30:18,524 --> 00:30:19,942
I'm gonna need
to borrow your truck.

434
00:30:20,192 --> 00:30:21,235
I'm coming with you.

435
00:30:21,319 --> 00:30:23,070
We don't know what kind of
condition she might be in.

436
00:30:23,279 --> 00:30:25,990
Miss, things could get
hairy out there.

437
00:30:29,035 --> 00:30:30,453
These are powerful sedatives.

438
00:30:30,619 --> 00:30:32,997
One too many, and she could
have respiratory failure.

439
00:30:33,080 --> 00:30:36,208
Also, I'm not as soft and witless
as your comment implies.

440
00:30:37,418 --> 00:30:38,919
Let's go, beefcake.

441
00:30:39,378 --> 00:30:40,379
Oh, Owen?

442
00:30:43,466 --> 00:30:44,925
Be careful, okay?

443
00:30:48,721 --> 00:30:50,097
If I don't make it back,

444
00:30:51,891 --> 00:30:54,977
remember, you're the one
who made me come.

445
00:30:57,438 --> 00:30:59,190
I'll be all right.

446
00:31:08,908 --> 00:31:11,619
We're not going to get any
closer to her in this thing.

447
00:31:12,995 --> 00:31:14,705
We should stop here.
Hold up.

448
00:31:17,208 --> 00:31:19,085
Load out, look alive,
stay alive.

449
00:31:19,668 --> 00:31:21,045
We got your back, brother.
Yeah.

450
00:31:23,005 --> 00:31:24,507
Claire, I'm
going to have to track her alone.

451
00:31:24,673 --> 00:31:27,009
I'll need you to be my eyes
if she runs. Copy?

452
00:31:27,176 --> 00:31:28,427
Copy that.

453
00:32:03,671 --> 00:32:06,590
Wheatley, I'm on fresh sign.
Wait for my signal.

454
00:32:33,117 --> 00:32:34,660
There you are.

455
00:32:49,383 --> 00:32:50,676
Hey, girl, you miss me?

456
00:32:53,554 --> 00:32:54,555
Easy. Hey!

457
00:32:54,930 --> 00:32:56,891
Hey.

458
00:32:58,225 --> 00:32:59,727
I brought you something.

459
00:33:05,232 --> 00:33:06,233
Here you go.

460
00:33:07,610 --> 00:33:08,819
That's right.

461
00:33:11,989 --> 00:33:13,073
Okay.

462
00:33:13,741 --> 00:33:15,868
Hey!

463
00:33:19,997 --> 00:33:21,040
You know me.

464
00:33:21,665 --> 00:33:23,459
Eyes on me.

465
00:33:25,211 --> 00:33:26,295
Yeah.

466
00:33:27,129 --> 00:33:29,256
Yeah. Yeah.

467
00:33:30,299 --> 00:33:32,176
You know me.

468
00:33:33,219 --> 00:33:34,345
That's right.

469
00:33:35,346 --> 00:33:36,764
That's right.

470
00:33:38,933 --> 00:33:39,934
Hold your...

471
00:33:42,436 --> 00:33:43,521
I told you to wait
for my signal.

472
00:33:48,359 --> 00:33:51,153
Back your men up right now.

473
00:34:02,373 --> 00:34:03,958
No, wait! No, no!
No, don't shoot her!

474
00:34:12,258 --> 00:34:14,176
Wheatley, you son of a bitch!

475
00:34:23,102 --> 00:34:24,270
Owen!

476
00:34:24,478 --> 00:34:25,771
What are you doing?

477
00:34:25,854 --> 00:34:26,855
Owen!

478
00:34:36,240 --> 00:34:37,366
You shoot me

479
00:34:38,075 --> 00:34:39,410
and that animal dies.

480
00:34:39,827 --> 00:34:41,704
I think we have
the drop on you, sweetheart.

481
00:34:41,870 --> 00:34:44,665
She's losing blood.
And if I don't treat her,

482
00:34:45,749 --> 00:34:47,376
she'll never make it
back to camp.

483
00:34:56,969 --> 00:34:58,012
How about this?

484
00:34:58,137 --> 00:35:00,681
That animal dies, I shoot you.

485
00:35:00,973 --> 00:35:02,558
You're gonna take care of her.

486
00:35:03,309 --> 00:35:04,310
Let's move out!

487
00:35:18,449 --> 00:35:21,035
Cut the cord and let's go!
Let's get out of here!

488
00:35:26,081 --> 00:35:28,500
No, no, no! Wait!

489
00:35:32,546 --> 00:35:34,048
Wait, are they protecting us?
What's going on?

490
00:35:36,300 --> 00:35:37,343
I don't think so.

491
00:35:40,387 --> 00:35:41,680
Why am I here?

492
00:35:45,934 --> 00:35:48,771
Hey, Mills, we got her done.
Mission accomplished.

493
00:35:51,774 --> 00:35:53,859
Just in the nick of time.

494
00:35:54,068 --> 00:35:55,653
Wheatley, what the hell
is going on there?

495
00:35:55,736 --> 00:35:57,071
We're a day
behind schedule already.

496
00:35:57,237 --> 00:35:59,823
I want that money in the bank
by the time I get back, all right?

497
00:35:59,990 --> 00:36:02,618
Okay, good. Listen to me.
No more delays, okay?

498
00:36:02,785 --> 00:36:03,911
If we don't get those animals.
. . Mr. Mills?

499
00:36:04,078 --> 00:36:05,496
Not now, Maisie. Thank you.
If we don't get

500
00:36:05,663 --> 00:36:06,914
those animals... Mr. Mills,
are the dinosaurs safe?

501
00:36:07,081 --> 00:36:08,749
I said not now!

502
00:36:10,959 --> 00:36:12,503
Mills? Mills?

503
00:36:12,670 --> 00:36:14,880
I'm sorry, honey.
This is an important call.

504
00:36:15,089 --> 00:36:17,883
If you go up to the library, I'll
meet you up there in a few minutes.

505
00:36:17,966 --> 00:36:18,967
Okay.
Okay.

506
00:36:19,134 --> 00:36:20,594
I'll tell you all about it,
I promise.

507
00:36:20,928 --> 00:36:22,471
Okay.
Okay.

508
00:36:23,597 --> 00:36:28,310
Wheatley, you get
those animals here now!

509
00:36:28,519 --> 00:36:30,604
I want that bonus.
We got the blue one.

510
00:36:30,688 --> 00:36:32,606
Bring her to me first!

511
00:36:32,815 --> 00:36:33,982
All right.

512
00:37:49,600 --> 00:37:51,226
No!

513
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:11,288
Hello, can anyone hear me?

514
00:38:11,705 --> 00:38:12,706
Hello?

515
00:38:12,998 --> 00:38:14,333
Hello, is anybody there?

516
00:38:14,666 --> 00:38:16,752
Can anybody copy?

517
00:38:18,462 --> 00:38:20,589
I'm... I'm trying everything.
It's jammed.

518
00:38:20,756 --> 00:38:22,466
"Just take the job, son,
build your people skills."

519
00:38:22,549 --> 00:38:23,592
Yeah, thanks, Dad.

520
00:38:25,677 --> 00:38:27,262
That's it.

521
00:38:29,681 --> 00:38:30,682
Proximity alert.

522
00:38:32,351 --> 00:38:33,727
Something's coming.

523
00:38:33,977 --> 00:38:35,103
Where does that tunnel lead?

524
00:38:36,230 --> 00:38:38,023
Well, it connects
to the rest of the...

525
00:38:39,399 --> 00:38:40,442
Park.

526
00:38:44,196 --> 00:38:46,949
It's the T. rex, it's the T. rex!
Claire, it's the T. rex.

527
00:38:47,032 --> 00:38:48,367
It's the T. rex,
it's the T. rex.

528
00:38:48,450 --> 00:38:49,785
Will you stop?
It's not the T. rex!

529
00:38:50,577 --> 00:38:51,954
Probably.

530
00:38:52,663 --> 00:38:53,956
"Probably"?

531
00:38:55,874 --> 00:38:59,294
Lava! Lava! Lava! Lava!

532
00:38:59,461 --> 00:39:00,921
Deep breaths, Franklin.

533
00:39:17,479 --> 00:39:18,981
See? Not a T. rex!

534
00:39:19,189 --> 00:39:20,399
How is this better?

535
00:39:31,702 --> 00:39:34,413
Claire, what do we do?
What do we do?

536
00:39:36,915 --> 00:39:37,958
It's stuck!

537
00:39:38,458 --> 00:39:39,459
Chair!

538
00:39:55,309 --> 00:39:57,102
Come on! Let's go!

539
00:39:58,437 --> 00:39:59,521
Come on, Franklin!

540
00:40:01,023 --> 00:40:03,358
We made it! Yeah!

541
00:40:23,837 --> 00:40:27,090
Come on, come on, come on!

542
00:40:29,676 --> 00:40:30,886
Yes, yes, go, go!

543
00:40:31,345 --> 00:40:32,346
Go, go!

544
00:40:44,066 --> 00:40:45,400
Claire!

545
00:40:46,985 --> 00:40:48,028
Holy...

546
00:40:59,957 --> 00:41:01,124
Run!

547
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:04,336
Run!

548
00:41:06,421 --> 00:41:07,506
Run!

549
00:41:17,516 --> 00:41:18,642
Run!

550
00:41:26,650 --> 00:41:27,943
Oh, my God!

551
00:42:19,619 --> 00:42:21,038
Go! Go!

552
00:42:21,204 --> 00:42:22,539
Okay.
We're okay.

553
00:42:22,748 --> 00:42:24,166
Buckle up.
It's okay.

554
00:42:25,167 --> 00:42:26,251
Get in!

555
00:43:45,747 --> 00:43:46,748
Owen!

556
00:44:39,467 --> 00:44:40,552
We gotta get out.

557
00:44:40,635 --> 00:44:42,971
We just fell
from the cliff. We're alive.

558
00:44:43,597 --> 00:44:44,806
We're alive!

559
00:44:44,890 --> 00:44:46,349
Franklin, move!

560
00:44:47,017 --> 00:44:48,476
No, no, no!

561
00:44:50,520 --> 00:44:53,356
No. Claire, we're gonna sink!
Water's leaking!

562
00:44:54,482 --> 00:44:57,861
Oh, God.
We need to get out!

563
00:45:00,864 --> 00:45:02,240
Franklin, watch out!

564
00:45:05,076 --> 00:45:07,412
I wanna go home!

565
00:45:10,957 --> 00:45:12,959
Owen. Owen!

566
00:45:13,084 --> 00:45:14,294
Owen?

567
00:45:14,461 --> 00:45:15,795
Owen. Okay.

568
00:45:17,130 --> 00:45:18,757
Move! Move!

569
00:45:23,303 --> 00:45:24,846
No! No, no, no!

570
00:45:25,889 --> 00:45:27,057
Push!

571
00:45:29,434 --> 00:45:31,603
Owen! No.

572
00:45:34,356 --> 00:45:35,690
Where's he going?

573
00:45:36,191 --> 00:45:37,400
Okay,
don't panic. Don't panic.

574
00:45:37,567 --> 00:45:39,986
We're running out of air.
We're not gonna make it.

575
00:45:40,153 --> 00:45:42,572
We are. We are.
Take a deep breath.

576
00:46:53,810 --> 00:46:54,811
Where is Zia?

577
00:46:54,936 --> 00:46:57,063
It was a double-cross. They
took her, she must be with Blue.

578
00:46:57,397 --> 00:46:59,190
Hey! Can you
still track them?

579
00:47:01,568 --> 00:47:02,736
Not anymore.

580
00:47:04,487 --> 00:47:05,572
It was a lie.

581
00:47:05,739 --> 00:47:07,574
It was a lie. Bastards!

582
00:47:07,699 --> 00:47:09,117
It was all a lie!

583
00:47:23,631 --> 00:47:24,924
Not all of it.

584
00:47:34,559 --> 00:47:37,520
Let's move it!
Move! Move!

585
00:47:37,896 --> 00:47:40,273
Anything of value,
I want on that boat!

586
00:47:40,440 --> 00:47:42,400
- How you doing?
- The bolt won't give!

587
00:47:42,567 --> 00:47:45,487
Just button it up,
and we'll tow it on! Let's go!

588
00:47:46,905 --> 00:47:48,698
Lock it up!
Move it out!

589
00:47:51,409 --> 00:47:53,536
Come on, now.

590
00:48:07,759 --> 00:48:10,595
Hey! Hey, hey, hey!
Hang on! Wait up!

591
00:48:10,970 --> 00:48:13,098
Stop that truck! Stop it!

592
00:48:16,142 --> 00:48:19,312
Hey, sweetheart.
Hello, hello.

593
00:48:27,612 --> 00:48:29,030
Beautiful.

594
00:48:29,322 --> 00:48:31,408
You're gonna feel that
when you wake up.

595
00:48:32,700 --> 00:48:34,953
Hey, put your weapon down!

596
00:48:35,161 --> 00:48:36,955
You got the barrel pointed
right at her head!

597
00:48:39,874 --> 00:48:41,251
They have Zia.
They're loading out.

598
00:48:41,459 --> 00:48:43,586
If they already had the
dinosaurs, why did they need us?

599
00:48:44,462 --> 00:48:46,840
They needed the tracking
system to capture Blue.

600
00:48:47,757 --> 00:48:49,050
There she is.
She don't look good.

601
00:48:54,013 --> 00:48:55,390
We need to get on that boat.

602
00:48:55,557 --> 00:48:57,183
The rock's good.
We're safe on the rock.

603
00:48:57,267 --> 00:48:58,893
It's the boat
or the lava, Franklin.

604
00:48:59,352 --> 00:49:01,187
All right, boat's good.
I'm all about the boat.

605
00:49:03,940 --> 00:49:05,608
Go, go, get on the boat!

606
00:49:05,775 --> 00:49:07,986
Get on the boat,
now! Go, go!

607
00:49:10,363 --> 00:49:12,532
- Move it, move it!
- Go, go, go!

608
00:49:15,577 --> 00:49:16,661
Get that truck!

609
00:49:34,512 --> 00:49:36,556
Franklin!
Hey, get that truck going!

610
00:49:36,639 --> 00:49:37,682
Okay.

611
00:49:38,516 --> 00:49:39,893
You okay?
Am I dead?

612
00:49:40,018 --> 00:49:41,227
Not yet, kid.

613
00:49:48,234 --> 00:49:49,444
Come on!

614
00:49:54,491 --> 00:49:57,160
Come on. Give me
your hand! Reach!

615
00:49:57,368 --> 00:49:58,620
Come on, reach!

616
00:50:10,590 --> 00:50:11,633
Hold on!

617
00:52:16,007 --> 00:52:17,008
Mr. Mills?

618
00:52:17,759 --> 00:52:19,302
Your visitor's waiting.

619
00:52:24,140 --> 00:52:25,141
Mr. Eversoll.

620
00:52:26,017 --> 00:52:28,519
It's a pleasure to meet you in person
after all this time. How are you?

621
00:52:28,728 --> 00:52:29,771
Excuse me.
Yeah?

622
00:52:29,854 --> 00:52:32,482
Where are the dinosaurs? The
dinosaurs are, uh, are en route.

623
00:52:33,191 --> 00:52:35,360
Oh, so, am I supposed
to sell these ones?

624
00:52:35,526 --> 00:52:37,403
You don't need to worry, okay?
They will be here soon.

625
00:52:38,446 --> 00:52:40,239
I don't really work
with amateurs,

626
00:52:40,323 --> 00:52:42,575
so I'm gonna contact my buyers
and call this off.

627
00:52:42,742 --> 00:52:45,203
They will be here tomorrow, and
your buyers won't be disappointed.

628
00:52:45,286 --> 00:52:46,663
Trust
me, whatever their interest,

629
00:52:46,746 --> 00:52:48,831
agricultural, industrial,
sport hunting,

630
00:52:48,998 --> 00:52:50,541
we will have something
to suit them.

631
00:52:50,708 --> 00:52:51,959
Eleven species,

632
00:52:52,126 --> 00:52:55,171
each with unique
biopharmaceutical properties.

633
00:52:55,338 --> 00:52:57,507
I estimate four million
per species.

634
00:52:58,257 --> 00:53:01,386
Four million is a slow
Tuesday where I'm from.

635
00:53:02,512 --> 00:53:03,638
You're wasting my time.

636
00:53:03,721 --> 00:53:05,306
You ever made 100 million
on a Tuesday?

637
00:53:05,390 --> 00:53:06,933
Huh?

638
00:53:08,685 --> 00:53:10,186
All this is in the past.

639
00:53:11,562 --> 00:53:13,356
I wanna talk to you
about the future.

640
00:53:14,857 --> 00:53:16,859
I'll give you 10 minutes.

641
00:53:18,945 --> 00:53:21,823
The whole point of selling
the Isla Nublar dinosaurs

642
00:53:21,906 --> 00:53:24,158
is to finance our future
operations here.

643
00:53:24,325 --> 00:53:26,494
It's, uh, seed money.

644
00:53:26,661 --> 00:53:29,747
Call it an overture to
something much more ambitious.

645
00:53:29,831 --> 00:53:32,208
Yeah, and more
lucrative, I guess.

646
00:53:32,375 --> 00:53:35,253
Right, we've reactivated
the old facilities,

647
00:53:35,461 --> 00:53:36,671
updated the technology,

648
00:53:36,879 --> 00:53:39,340
and imported the best geneticists
from around the world.

649
00:53:39,549 --> 00:53:41,509
This operation has been
many years in the planning.

650
00:53:41,718 --> 00:53:44,011
Genetic power is
an uncharted frontier.

651
00:53:44,929 --> 00:53:48,057
The potential for growth
is more than you can fathom.

652
00:53:50,768 --> 00:53:51,894
This way, please.

653
00:53:52,353 --> 00:53:54,397
If the entire run
of our sorry history

654
00:53:54,522 --> 00:53:56,816
has taught us
one irrevocable lesson,

655
00:53:56,899 --> 00:54:00,153
it's that man is inevitably
drawn to war

656
00:54:00,319 --> 00:54:03,781
and is willing to use any
means necessary to win it.

657
00:54:04,449 --> 00:54:05,908
You're gonna weaponize them?

658
00:54:06,117 --> 00:54:07,994
We've been using animals
in combat for centuries.

659
00:54:08,161 --> 00:54:09,746
Horses, elephants.

660
00:54:09,954 --> 00:54:11,956
The Soviets used
disease-bearing rats

661
00:54:12,039 --> 00:54:13,249
against the Germans
at Stalingrad.

662
00:54:13,332 --> 00:54:14,333
Yes, yes.

663
00:54:14,542 --> 00:54:16,169
Our geneticists have created

664
00:54:16,294 --> 00:54:19,172
a direct descendant
of Henry Wu's masterpiece,

665
00:54:19,338 --> 00:54:21,174
the animal that took down
Jurassic World.

666
00:54:22,633 --> 00:54:23,926
Indominus rex.

667
00:54:24,177 --> 00:54:27,263
Her DNA, retrieved from the island
way before its destruction,

668
00:54:27,430 --> 00:54:31,684
forms the architecture
of a completely new creature.

669
00:54:31,851 --> 00:54:35,188
Every bone and muscle designed
for hunting and killing.

670
00:54:35,396 --> 00:54:37,774
And thanks
to Owen Grady's research,

671
00:54:37,940 --> 00:54:40,193
it follows human command.

672
00:54:40,651 --> 00:54:43,863
We call it the Indoraptor.

673
00:54:50,995 --> 00:54:52,038
Grandpa?

674
00:54:53,247 --> 00:54:54,248
Grandpa?

675
00:55:12,099 --> 00:55:14,435
Grandpa.

676
00:55:14,977 --> 00:55:17,021
Maisie, what are you doing up?

677
00:55:17,104 --> 00:55:20,024
There was a man here
today with Mr. Mills.

678
00:55:20,233 --> 00:55:23,402
It was probably just some business
about the sanctuary. That's all.

679
00:55:23,569 --> 00:55:24,737
I heard them talking.

680
00:55:24,904 --> 00:55:26,113
They're going to sell
the dinosaurs.

681
00:55:26,614 --> 00:55:28,783
They're bringing them here.
Oh. Huh.

682
00:55:28,866 --> 00:55:30,117
I'm sure you misunderstood.

683
00:55:30,201 --> 00:55:31,494
I know what I heard, Grandpa.

684
00:55:31,577 --> 00:55:34,038
Maisie, it's way past
your bedtime.

685
00:55:34,956 --> 00:55:36,332
Let's talk about this
in the morning.

686
00:55:36,624 --> 00:55:37,834
But...
Maisie,

687
00:55:38,125 --> 00:55:39,460
I'll find out tomorrow.

688
00:55:39,919 --> 00:55:41,045
Go to bed.

689
00:55:43,047 --> 00:55:44,048
Good night.

690
00:55:44,131 --> 00:55:45,633
Good night, my heart.

691
00:56:14,996 --> 00:56:16,706
Jesus.

692
00:56:24,589 --> 00:56:26,007
It's okay, Blue,
just a little longer.

693
00:56:27,383 --> 00:56:28,634
Oh, my God!
You guys are alive!

694
00:56:33,890 --> 00:56:35,308
Oh, look what
they've done to her.

695
00:56:35,808 --> 00:56:36,851
Who are these assholes?

696
00:56:36,934 --> 00:56:38,811
Animal traffickers. Look
how they're treating them.

697
00:56:38,936 --> 00:56:41,147
They're not gonna take them to a
sanctuary, they're gonna sell them.

698
00:56:41,314 --> 00:56:42,982
Not Blue, they need her
for something else.

699
00:56:43,149 --> 00:56:44,233
Like what?

700
00:56:44,317 --> 00:56:45,484
I don't know, but she's...

701
00:56:45,693 --> 00:56:47,486
She's hemorrhaging,
and I don't have instruments

702
00:56:47,612 --> 00:56:48,779
and they want to keep her alive.

703
00:56:48,988 --> 00:56:49,989
Hey.

704
00:56:51,866 --> 00:56:53,451
- You're all right, you're all right.
- Claire, come here.

705
00:56:53,701 --> 00:56:55,119
Come on.
Put your hand here.

706
00:56:55,244 --> 00:56:56,245
Steady pressure.

707
00:56:56,370 --> 00:56:57,872
Hey, hey, hey.

708
00:56:59,749 --> 00:57:00,750
Watch out.

709
00:57:01,083 --> 00:57:03,836
I can't take the bullet out without
a transfusion from another animal.

710
00:57:04,045 --> 00:57:05,463
Which one of you knows
how to find a vein?

711
00:57:05,630 --> 00:57:07,715
Oh! I did a blood drive
for the Red Cross.

712
00:57:08,257 --> 00:57:10,009
Great. Okay.

713
00:57:10,718 --> 00:57:12,511
Franklin, take over for Claire.

714
00:57:12,678 --> 00:57:13,763
No, no, no.

715
00:57:13,971 --> 00:57:15,514
Franklin, now!

716
00:57:17,725 --> 00:57:19,810
Steady pressure.

717
00:57:19,894 --> 00:57:22,396
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

718
00:57:22,605 --> 00:57:23,606
Is it in my mouth?

719
00:57:23,773 --> 00:57:25,066
Did it get in my mouth?
Mmm-mmm.

720
00:57:25,232 --> 00:57:26,442
You sure?
You're good.

721
00:57:26,651 --> 00:57:27,652
I can feel it on my face.

722
00:57:27,735 --> 00:57:28,861
All of the animals
should be sedated.

723
00:57:29,028 --> 00:57:30,196
Look for any kind of Tetanuran,

724
00:57:30,363 --> 00:57:32,114
their blood type
should be close enough.

725
00:57:32,281 --> 00:57:35,368
Look for carnivores with two or
three fingers. No more than three.

726
00:57:37,495 --> 00:57:39,413
I think there's one on board.

727
00:57:54,220 --> 00:57:55,721
You gotta be kidding me.

728
00:58:12,697 --> 00:58:13,823
She's tranqued.

729
00:58:19,412 --> 00:58:21,789
Okay, okay. Okay,
I got it. Come on.

730
00:58:22,248 --> 00:58:24,375
Okay.

731
00:58:28,254 --> 00:58:30,881
Oh, no. Are you okay?

732
00:58:32,091 --> 00:58:33,092
I'm okay.

733
00:58:33,759 --> 00:58:35,136
Come on.

734
00:58:40,683 --> 00:58:42,184
You're gonna have to do it.
I can't.

735
00:58:42,393 --> 00:58:43,436
The skin is too thick.

736
00:58:43,519 --> 00:58:45,187
I have to use both my hands
to put pressure on the vein.

737
00:58:45,563 --> 00:58:46,564
You gotta do it.

738
00:58:46,689 --> 00:58:48,107
I... I can't...
I can't reach.

739
00:58:48,274 --> 00:58:50,401
You're gonna have to
climb up there.

740
00:58:51,527 --> 00:58:52,611
Oh, I'm not...

741
00:58:52,695 --> 00:58:53,821
No, no. I'm not gonna
climb up there.

742
00:58:54,030 --> 00:58:55,573
You'll be okay.
It's like riding a bull.

743
00:58:55,698 --> 00:58:57,742
"Riding a bull"?
I didn't grow up at a rodeo

744
00:58:57,950 --> 00:58:59,618
or wherever you came from.
She's asleep now.

745
00:58:59,785 --> 00:59:02,872
I can't say that she's gonna be
asleep a minute from now, so get up.

746
00:59:03,080 --> 00:59:03,956
Okay, fine.

747
00:59:04,123 --> 00:59:06,459
Oh, my God, she stinks.

748
00:59:07,043 --> 00:59:08,836
Gently. Gently.

749
00:59:08,919 --> 00:59:10,296
No, no, no.

750
00:59:20,347 --> 00:59:21,348
Good job.

751
00:59:21,891 --> 00:59:24,143
You're making this
look totally normal.

752
00:59:26,604 --> 00:59:29,482
You're gonna have to jam it in
there, it's really thick skin.

753
00:59:30,274 --> 00:59:31,317
Ready?
Yeah.

754
00:59:31,525 --> 00:59:33,694
Okay. One, two...
One, two...

755
00:59:33,903 --> 00:59:35,821
Stab!

756
00:59:36,489 --> 00:59:37,490
Owen!

757
00:59:46,540 --> 00:59:47,541
It's working.

758
00:59:51,128 --> 00:59:52,213
The pee is silent.

759
00:59:52,421 --> 00:59:53,631
That door is open.

760
00:59:53,798 --> 00:59:54,799
I got it.

761
01:00:13,109 --> 01:00:14,985
The bars!
The bars, Owen.

762
01:00:15,069 --> 01:00:17,321
Owen, I can get
through there. Come on.

763
01:00:30,793 --> 01:00:32,002
Jump, Owen!

764
01:00:38,592 --> 01:00:40,886
Owen, jump. Now!

765
01:00:50,688 --> 01:00:51,897
Did you see that?

766
01:00:52,648 --> 01:00:53,649
Uh-huh.

767
01:00:54,275 --> 01:00:55,818
Please tell me
you got the blood.

768
01:02:20,569 --> 01:02:22,780
This is Delta. She's one
of the holdover theropods,

769
01:02:22,863 --> 01:02:24,073
one of the survivors
from the second group.

770
01:02:25,199 --> 01:02:26,325
Now, watch this.

771
01:02:26,492 --> 01:02:29,078
I show any sign of weakness
whatsoever...

772
01:02:35,334 --> 01:02:36,543
See that?

773
01:02:36,752 --> 01:02:40,422
Day 176. Blue is showing
unprecedented levels of compliance.

774
01:02:41,090 --> 01:02:42,341
Here I am with Blue.

775
01:02:43,884 --> 01:02:45,636
She's a theropod
from the new group.

776
01:02:46,762 --> 01:02:49,014
I show signs of weakness...

777
01:03:02,903 --> 01:03:03,904
Hey.

778
01:03:05,364 --> 01:03:06,615
I'm okay.

779
01:03:09,076 --> 01:03:10,244
She's pretty extraordinary.

780
01:03:22,214 --> 01:03:24,174
Blue, displaying levels

781
01:03:24,383 --> 01:03:27,177
of interest, concern,

782
01:03:27,386 --> 01:03:28,846
hyper-intelligence,

783
01:03:29,430 --> 01:03:30,889
cognitive bonding.

784
01:03:31,056 --> 01:03:32,141
See that?

785
01:03:33,600 --> 01:03:36,020
She's tilting her head,
she's craning forward.

786
01:03:37,021 --> 01:03:39,273
Increased eye movement.
She's curious.

787
01:03:39,857 --> 01:03:41,317
She's showing empathy.

788
01:03:42,484 --> 01:03:45,904
Okay, I'm gonna make an incision
in her leg to remove the bullet.

789
01:03:50,200 --> 01:03:52,077
It's a lot of muscle tissue.

790
01:04:24,985 --> 01:04:25,986
She's gonna be okay.

791
01:04:32,826 --> 01:04:33,827
Blue is the key.

792
01:04:36,121 --> 01:04:38,332
That's it. You have Blue,

793
01:04:38,832 --> 01:04:40,542
you get these raptors to do anything.

794
01:04:40,709 --> 01:04:41,794
You're sure she'll live?

795
01:04:41,960 --> 01:04:43,295
If it dies, we have
blood samples.

796
01:04:43,379 --> 01:04:44,922
No, no, no.
That's not good enough.

797
01:04:45,130 --> 01:04:48,217
The raptor is a behavioral specimen.
We need her in good health.

798
01:04:48,384 --> 01:04:52,221
I didn't shoot the damn thing.
What do you want me to do, huh?

799
01:04:52,971 --> 01:04:57,434
You don't have the faintest
comprehension of what I'm doing here.

800
01:04:57,893 --> 01:05:02,106
Do you understand the complexity of
creating an entirely new life form?

801
01:05:02,231 --> 01:05:05,150
No, but I understand the
complexity of paying for it, okay?

802
01:05:05,567 --> 01:05:09,071
All your money will have been wasted if
I don't get Blue here in good health.

803
01:05:09,446 --> 01:05:11,907
To get the next iteration
under control,

804
01:05:12,032 --> 01:05:15,536
it needs to form a familial bond
with a closely related genetic link.

805
01:05:15,702 --> 01:05:17,871
English, Henry.
It needs a mother!

806
01:05:20,207 --> 01:05:24,169
Blue's DNA will be part of the
next Indoraptor's makeup.

807
01:05:24,378 --> 01:05:27,423
So, it will be genetically coded
to recognize her authority

808
01:05:27,589 --> 01:05:28,715
and assume her traits.

809
01:05:28,882 --> 01:05:30,050
Empathy. Obedience.

810
01:05:30,217 --> 01:05:32,511
Everything the prototype
you have now is missing.

811
01:05:32,719 --> 01:05:34,596
Okay, so how long is this gonna take?

812
01:05:34,763 --> 01:05:37,057
It's not a sprint, Mr. Mills.
It's a marathon.

813
01:05:37,266 --> 01:05:38,642
A marathon sounds expensive.

814
01:05:39,268 --> 01:05:42,271
Besides, time is running out,
and so is my patience, Henry.

815
01:05:42,438 --> 01:05:43,689
You have to understand,

816
01:05:43,856 --> 01:05:45,566
this is all uncharted territory.

817
01:05:45,649 --> 01:05:46,942
A wolf, genetically,

818
01:05:47,151 --> 01:05:49,153
is barely distinguishable
from a bulldog,

819
01:05:49,820 --> 01:05:51,947
but within that gray area

820
01:05:53,740 --> 01:05:54,950
is art.

821
01:05:55,117 --> 01:05:57,119
Spare me the poetry, Henry.

822
01:05:57,202 --> 01:05:59,163
Can you do it? Can you do it?

823
01:05:59,329 --> 01:06:00,497
Yes, I can do it.

824
01:06:01,290 --> 01:06:02,833
Well, then do it.

825
01:06:03,000 --> 01:06:04,293
Thank you.

826
01:06:04,835 --> 01:06:06,462
God, you're a pain in the ass.

827
01:06:19,683 --> 01:06:21,685
Hey. Hey! Maisie!

828
01:06:24,688 --> 01:06:27,191
What is that? What is it?

829
01:06:34,823 --> 01:06:37,367
Keep her in there
and keep the door locked.

830
01:06:37,784 --> 01:06:38,994
You want to keep her locked in?

831
01:06:39,119 --> 01:06:40,454
That's exactly what I want.

832
01:06:40,621 --> 01:06:42,581
Sir Benjamin needs
to see you now.

833
01:06:45,959 --> 01:06:47,753
I believe it's important.

834
01:07:18,534 --> 01:07:21,078
All crew, prepare for docking.

835
01:07:21,245 --> 01:07:22,996
All crew, prepare for docking.

836
01:07:23,163 --> 01:07:24,331
Let's move it, boys.

837
01:07:24,498 --> 01:07:27,000
Jeez! Will somebody shut that
thing up? It sounds like my ex-wife.

838
01:07:27,125 --> 01:07:28,377
We're here.

839
01:07:28,544 --> 01:07:29,545
Where?

840
01:07:29,628 --> 01:07:31,171
Just get
in your truck and let's go.

841
01:07:31,505 --> 01:07:32,839
Move. Move!
Move, move, move!

842
01:07:34,007 --> 01:07:35,342
You got a heartbeat?

843
01:07:35,467 --> 01:07:36,927
Yeah. Do you?

844
01:07:37,135 --> 01:07:38,178
I need blood samples.

845
01:07:38,303 --> 01:07:39,972
I'm not here to help you
reset the food chain,

846
01:07:40,055 --> 01:07:42,182
so take your own damn samples.

847
01:07:46,103 --> 01:07:47,646
What a nasty woman.

848
01:07:47,854 --> 01:07:49,314
Go, go, now, now. Go.

849
01:07:50,315 --> 01:07:53,402
Attention, all crew.
Attention, all crew.

850
01:07:53,902 --> 01:07:56,238
Drivers, get to your vehicles.

851
01:07:57,030 --> 01:07:58,031
Hey!

852
01:07:59,157 --> 01:08:00,826
What the hell is going on?
I was...

853
01:08:02,077 --> 01:08:03,745
I needed an extra pair
of hands. He volunteered.

854
01:08:04,162 --> 01:08:05,872
Are you deck crew?
Aye, aye.

855
01:08:06,039 --> 01:08:07,499
We're loading out.
Follow me.

856
01:08:07,708 --> 01:08:09,293
Uh, does that mean we're
leaving the ship right now?

857
01:08:09,459 --> 01:08:11,962
That is what "loading out" means.
Now get moving. Come on, follow me.

858
01:08:12,963 --> 01:08:14,798
Come on, kid. There's lots to do.
Shit!

859
01:08:15,007 --> 01:08:17,050
Oh, no. He's not gonna make it.
Get a move on. Come on.

860
01:08:17,217 --> 01:08:19,428
- Move it! Come on. Move!
- We have to help him.

861
01:08:19,636 --> 01:08:20,971
Stay here, I'll get him.

862
01:08:22,681 --> 01:08:24,182
There's no time.
Let's go.

863
01:08:30,230 --> 01:08:31,523
I got five. I'm on it.

864
01:08:34,192 --> 01:08:35,694
Why is this truck still here?

865
01:08:38,947 --> 01:08:39,948
Good to go!

866
01:08:40,115 --> 01:08:41,158
Roll out!

867
01:08:42,826 --> 01:08:43,910
Where are they taking them?

868
01:08:44,703 --> 01:08:46,330
- We're about to find out.
- Let's go!

869
01:08:47,914 --> 01:08:48,915
Ready to roll!

870
01:08:49,082 --> 01:08:50,250
Keep it coming!

871
01:08:52,878 --> 01:08:54,171
Move it out!
Keep it rolling!

872
01:08:56,632 --> 01:08:57,966
Good to go!

873
01:08:58,175 --> 01:08:59,384
Come on, girl.

874
01:09:06,516 --> 01:09:07,559
Yes!

875
01:09:15,525 --> 01:09:17,402
This is Lockwood's estate.

876
01:09:19,112 --> 01:09:21,198
He must have one big-ass garage.

877
01:09:35,671 --> 01:09:38,256
Let's go! We want
this offloaded in 10 mikes!

878
01:09:39,091 --> 01:09:40,092
Big door's open.

879
01:10:07,452 --> 01:10:10,330
Did you really think
you could get away with it?

880
01:10:11,623 --> 01:10:13,208
In my own house.

881
01:10:13,375 --> 01:10:14,918
You entrusted me

882
01:10:15,752 --> 01:10:18,672
to guide your fortune
into the future.

883
01:10:19,339 --> 01:10:21,258
I have done that.
Damn you!

884
01:10:25,262 --> 01:10:26,513
Pick up that phone.

885
01:10:26,930 --> 01:10:29,933
I want you to call the police.

886
01:10:30,600 --> 01:10:33,061
It'll be easier
if the story comes from you.

887
01:10:35,605 --> 01:10:37,023
As you say, sir.

888
01:10:44,573 --> 01:10:46,950
You know, I've been thinking,
John Hammond was right.

889
01:10:49,745 --> 01:10:52,789
It was an unholy thing
that you did.

890
01:10:56,001 --> 01:10:58,712
I'm not the only guilty one
here, am I, sir?

891
01:11:18,482 --> 01:11:19,483
Hey.

892
01:11:21,234 --> 01:11:23,862
We hit that town, we call the
cavalry, shut this down.

893
01:11:28,533 --> 01:11:30,076
Whoo. Hello.

894
01:11:30,869 --> 01:11:32,996
You should have stayed
on the island.

895
01:11:33,205 --> 01:11:34,247
Better odds.

896
01:11:45,842 --> 01:11:46,885
Hi, Claire.

897
01:11:48,762 --> 01:11:50,847
I just wanted to come
and apologize.

898
01:11:51,431 --> 01:11:53,183
I didn't wanna bring you
into any of this,

899
01:11:53,391 --> 01:11:55,310
but it was the only way
that we could get the raptor.

900
01:11:56,686 --> 01:11:57,938
We needed it.

901
01:11:58,271 --> 01:11:59,272
Come on.

902
01:12:00,941 --> 01:12:03,026
So, what? This is it?

903
01:12:03,985 --> 01:12:05,987
I mean, you're a smart guy,
you could have

904
01:12:06,112 --> 01:12:07,656
started a foundation,
cured cancer,

905
01:12:07,823 --> 01:12:08,990
but instead you,

906
01:12:10,033 --> 01:12:11,034
what?

907
01:12:12,077 --> 01:12:13,245
Sell endangered species?

908
01:12:13,453 --> 01:12:14,663
I saved these animals.

909
01:12:14,871 --> 01:12:16,456
You betrayed a dying man
for money.

910
01:12:16,873 --> 01:12:19,042
Claire, I admire your idealism,

911
01:12:19,209 --> 01:12:20,836
but we both exploited
these animals.

912
01:12:20,961 --> 01:12:22,879
At least I have the integrity
to admit it.

913
01:12:23,088 --> 01:12:24,965
I never, ever did anything
remotely illegal.

914
01:12:25,048 --> 01:12:27,342
You authorized the creation
of the Indominus rex.

915
01:12:28,510 --> 01:12:32,180
You exploited a living
thing in a cage for money.

916
01:12:32,305 --> 01:12:34,307
How is that different? Huh?

917
01:12:34,391 --> 01:12:37,936
And you, the man who proved
raptors can follow orders.

918
01:12:38,144 --> 01:12:40,730
You never thought about the
applications of your research, Owen?

919
01:12:40,939 --> 01:12:43,650
How many millions a trained
predator might be worth?

920
01:12:46,820 --> 01:12:47,863
You two,

921
01:12:48,613 --> 01:12:50,699
you're the parents
of the new world.

922
01:12:53,410 --> 01:12:55,161
Hey! Let him go.

923
01:12:55,579 --> 01:12:57,497
Owen.
I think I'll break it.

924
01:12:57,706 --> 01:12:58,874
Let him go.

925
01:13:02,961 --> 01:13:04,921
Claire, I just want...

926
01:13:13,013 --> 01:13:14,306
Say, how we gonna end this?

927
01:13:14,472 --> 01:13:18,894
Well, as far as everybody else is
concerned, they burned up on the island.

928
01:13:35,869 --> 01:13:38,705
Buyers on approach.
Looks like a full house.

929
01:13:39,414 --> 01:13:40,415
Roger that.

930
01:14:07,651 --> 01:14:09,069
- Good evening.
- Good evening.

931
01:14:09,319 --> 01:14:10,445
- Welcome, gentlemen.
- Good evening.

932
01:14:10,612 --> 01:14:11,655
Welcome.

933
01:14:13,448 --> 01:14:17,035
And these guys are development
rep for Aldaris Pharmaceuticals.

934
01:14:17,118 --> 01:14:18,119
Great.

935
01:14:18,203 --> 01:14:20,872
The guy in the beard is a
proxy for Gregor Adlrich,

936
01:14:21,039 --> 01:14:22,624
a Slovenian arms dealer.

937
01:14:24,209 --> 01:14:26,086
Equine buyers.

938
01:14:26,252 --> 01:14:29,005
Rand Magnus, an oil magnate
from Houston.

939
01:14:29,172 --> 01:14:30,340
What's their interest?

940
01:14:30,507 --> 01:14:31,716
Strictly personal.

941
01:14:32,342 --> 01:14:35,345
His kid wants
a baby Triceratops.

942
01:14:36,221 --> 01:14:37,555
Glenn, how's Janet?

943
01:14:37,722 --> 01:14:39,224
She's doing fine, thanks.

944
01:14:39,307 --> 01:14:41,226
Straight ahead, Glenn.

945
01:14:57,951 --> 01:15:00,745
Well, he only
wants carnivores. Two of them.

946
01:15:01,329 --> 01:15:03,790
Looks like Lockwood's gonna
have a very good night.

947
01:15:04,290 --> 01:15:05,458
Will he be joining us?

948
01:15:05,792 --> 01:15:07,711
Um...
That's very unlikely.

949
01:15:20,557 --> 01:15:21,558
Grandpa?

950
01:15:29,566 --> 01:15:30,567
Grandpa?

951
01:15:31,901 --> 01:15:33,236
Grandpa, wake up.

952
01:15:35,530 --> 01:15:36,573
Grandpa!

953
01:15:38,491 --> 01:15:40,243
Grandpa, wake up.
Wake up.

954
01:15:40,410 --> 01:15:42,537
Iris!

955
01:15:56,301 --> 01:15:57,385
Iris!

956
01:16:00,472 --> 01:16:01,681
It's over.

957
01:16:04,809 --> 01:16:05,810
What a tragedy.

958
01:16:10,774 --> 01:16:12,776
I suppose now he's gone,

959
01:16:13,234 --> 01:16:14,944
you'll be looking
for another situation.

960
01:16:15,111 --> 01:16:16,112
No.

961
01:16:17,447 --> 01:16:18,448
Maisie needs me.

962
01:16:20,909 --> 01:16:22,952
I'm her guardian now.

963
01:16:24,412 --> 01:16:26,206
What she needs
is no longer your concern.

964
01:16:28,124 --> 01:16:30,627
You don't understand her
the way I do.

965
01:16:31,836 --> 01:16:33,421
I understand her value.

966
01:16:35,340 --> 01:16:36,883
But I raised her.

967
01:16:37,675 --> 01:16:40,011
I raised both of them.

968
01:16:44,724 --> 01:16:46,810
- Please, Mr. Mills.
- Goodbye, Iris.

969
01:17:27,058 --> 01:17:30,812
Do you remember the first time
you saw a dinosaur?

970
01:17:33,356 --> 01:17:35,859
First time you see them,
it's like

971
01:17:36,025 --> 01:17:37,152
a miracle.

972
01:17:37,861 --> 01:17:40,697
You read about them in books,
you see the bones in museums,

973
01:17:40,822 --> 01:17:43,408
but you don't really believe it.

974
01:17:43,575 --> 01:17:45,410
They're like myths.

975
01:17:46,077 --> 01:17:47,537
And then you see

976
01:17:48,955 --> 01:17:50,832
that first one alive.

977
01:17:56,045 --> 01:17:57,505
This is not your fault.

978
01:17:58,423 --> 01:17:59,424
But it is.

979
01:17:59,591 --> 01:18:00,842
No.

980
01:18:02,552 --> 01:18:04,053
This one's on me.

981
01:18:06,681 --> 01:18:08,683
I showed them the way.

982
01:18:11,019 --> 01:18:12,061
Listen.

983
01:18:12,187 --> 01:18:15,481
You and I are gonna have plenty
of time to talk about this later.

984
01:18:15,565 --> 01:18:16,566
If there is a later.

985
01:18:17,025 --> 01:18:18,443
Yes, there is.

986
01:18:20,820 --> 01:18:22,322
I got a cabin to finish.

987
01:18:31,039 --> 01:18:32,081
What is that?

988
01:18:36,753 --> 01:18:39,172
Well, look who just woke up.

989
01:18:40,715 --> 01:18:42,592
Go.

990
01:18:50,350 --> 01:18:51,809
We're getting out of here.

991
01:18:53,478 --> 01:18:57,065
Welcome, ladies, gentlemen,

992
01:18:57,607 --> 01:19:00,985
to this extraordinary evening.

993
01:19:01,152 --> 01:19:02,820
Let's dive right in

994
01:19:02,987 --> 01:19:04,822
with lot number one,

995
01:19:06,199 --> 01:19:08,493
the Ankylosaurus.

996
01:19:10,119 --> 01:19:12,413
She's a herbivorous quadruped,

997
01:19:12,830 --> 01:19:14,540
late Cretaceous.

998
01:19:14,958 --> 01:19:18,419
This is one of the largest
armored dinosaurs,

999
01:19:19,379 --> 01:19:23,174
known by paleontologists
as a living tank.

1000
01:19:29,681 --> 01:19:31,015
What are you doing?

1001
01:19:31,182 --> 01:19:32,392
Escaping.

1002
01:19:32,558 --> 01:19:33,726
You sure about this?

1003
01:19:33,893 --> 01:19:34,936
Nope.

1004
01:19:37,021 --> 01:19:39,941
I have $4 million, anyone five?

1005
01:19:40,191 --> 01:19:41,776
Five.
$5 million.

1006
01:19:41,985 --> 01:19:43,403
$5 million. Do I hear six?

1007
01:19:43,611 --> 01:19:45,238
- $6 million.
- $6 million, thank you.

1008
01:19:45,446 --> 01:19:47,865
Any increase on six?
Seven. $7 million.

1009
01:19:48,074 --> 01:19:48,992
$8 million.

1010
01:19:49,075 --> 01:19:51,786
Nine million on the phones.
Do I hear 10 million?

1011
01:19:51,995 --> 01:19:53,454
10 million? 10 million.
10.

1012
01:19:53,663 --> 01:19:55,623
$10 million! Once, twice...

1013
01:19:56,541 --> 01:19:57,667
Sold!

1014
01:19:58,584 --> 01:20:01,004
To the gentleman from Indonesia.
Congratulations, sir.

1015
01:20:06,301 --> 01:20:09,178
Lot number two,
ladies and gentlemen.

1016
01:20:10,138 --> 01:20:12,307
A juvenile Allosaurus.

1017
01:20:13,808 --> 01:20:17,812
A fierce
and an aggressive predator.

1018
01:20:22,108 --> 01:20:23,276
Sold!

1019
01:20:23,651 --> 01:20:24,360
Sold!

1020
01:20:25,862 --> 01:20:27,071
Sold.

1021
01:20:29,824 --> 01:20:30,825
Wonderful.

1022
01:20:31,326 --> 01:20:33,578
Wonderful bidding.

1023
01:20:46,883 --> 01:20:48,885
Come on. You're good.
Let's go.

1024
01:20:49,385 --> 01:20:50,678
- Move it out.
- Here we go.

1025
01:20:50,762 --> 01:20:51,971
Bring it on back. Let's go.

1026
01:21:01,606 --> 01:21:02,607
Okay.

1027
01:21:03,483 --> 01:21:06,069
Hey! Hey, you. Look at me!
Look at me!

1028
01:21:07,111 --> 01:21:08,112
Hey!

1029
01:21:08,738 --> 01:21:10,239
Come with me.
Come with me!

1030
01:21:10,615 --> 01:21:11,991
Okay, there you go.

1031
01:21:30,134 --> 01:21:31,427
You're welcome.

1032
01:21:40,311 --> 01:21:41,771
Hey, wait!

1033
01:21:42,188 --> 01:21:43,481
Please, please wait.

1034
01:21:48,403 --> 01:21:50,113
That's Lockwood's granddaughter.

1035
01:21:51,989 --> 01:21:54,992
Hey. Hey, kid. You wanna
come down out of there?

1036
01:21:57,787 --> 01:21:59,205
Uh...
Uh...

1037
01:22:00,373 --> 01:22:01,874
Do you remember me?

1038
01:22:03,876 --> 01:22:04,877
Yeah?

1039
01:22:05,753 --> 01:22:07,922
My name is Claire.
What's yours?

1040
01:22:09,048 --> 01:22:10,216
Maisie.

1041
01:22:10,716 --> 01:22:11,759
Maisie Lockwood.

1042
01:22:11,926 --> 01:22:13,094
Maisie.

1043
01:22:13,261 --> 01:22:15,972
This is my friend, Owen.

1044
01:22:17,265 --> 01:22:18,391
I saw you

1045
01:22:18,558 --> 01:22:21,269
with the Velociraptors.
Blue.

1046
01:22:21,352 --> 01:22:22,854
Oh. Oh, yeah?

1047
01:22:23,896 --> 01:22:24,939
Uh...

1048
01:22:25,815 --> 01:22:27,567
You like dinosaurs?

1049
01:22:29,902 --> 01:22:30,903
So do I.

1050
01:22:31,070 --> 01:22:32,864
Tell you what,
you come on down from there,

1051
01:22:32,947 --> 01:22:34,782
I'll tell you everything
you need to know about Blue.

1052
01:22:35,825 --> 01:22:37,076
That sound all right?

1053
01:22:38,494 --> 01:22:39,787
All right, yeah.

1054
01:22:39,954 --> 01:22:41,289
Come on down.

1055
01:22:50,047 --> 01:22:51,132
Sweetie,

1056
01:22:51,841 --> 01:22:53,843
we need some help
finding your grandfather.

1057
01:22:53,968 --> 01:22:55,303
Can you take us to him?

1058
01:22:55,386 --> 01:22:57,430
No.

1059
01:22:58,055 --> 01:22:59,724
You made it down here
all by yourself, huh?

1060
01:23:00,099 --> 01:23:01,392
Brave kid.

1061
01:23:01,767 --> 01:23:03,394
Looks like you could
use a friend.

1062
01:23:04,353 --> 01:23:05,354
He's gone.

1063
01:23:10,568 --> 01:23:12,028
Listen, I'll tell you what,

1064
01:23:12,195 --> 01:23:14,322
we were just about to go
and find our friends

1065
01:23:14,405 --> 01:23:15,948
and then get
the heck out of here.

1066
01:23:16,407 --> 01:23:17,909
You wanna come with us?

1067
01:23:18,326 --> 01:23:20,077
We could use a friend, too.

1068
01:23:22,205 --> 01:23:23,706
- 19.5!
- 19. 5.

1069
01:23:23,873 --> 01:23:24,999
Do I hear 20?

1070
01:23:25,458 --> 01:23:28,044
Do I hear 20? 20? Thank you.
Thank you.

1071
01:23:28,211 --> 01:23:29,754
20. Do I hear...

1072
01:23:29,921 --> 01:23:32,507
$21 million!

1073
01:23:32,673 --> 01:23:34,717
21 million...

1074
01:23:35,051 --> 01:23:36,594
Sold!

1075
01:23:36,761 --> 01:23:38,346
And now, ladies and gentlemen,

1076
01:23:38,513 --> 01:23:41,807
that we are halfway through
the evening...

1077
01:23:43,476 --> 01:23:46,812
...we'd like to offer
a special treat to our

1078
01:23:47,438 --> 01:23:49,607
discriminating buyers.

1079
01:23:50,525 --> 01:23:52,276
This evening we will preview

1080
01:23:53,236 --> 01:23:56,322
a new asset that
we've been developing.

1081
01:23:57,031 --> 01:23:58,908
A creature of the future

1082
01:23:59,075 --> 01:24:01,619
made from pieces of the past.

1083
01:24:03,746 --> 01:24:05,748
Ladies and gentlemen,
please be warned,

1084
01:24:06,582 --> 01:24:10,419
this is the perfect blend of the
two most dangerous creatures

1085
01:24:10,503 --> 01:24:12,797
that have ever walked the earth.

1086
01:24:13,881 --> 01:24:17,426
We call it the Indoraptor.

1087
01:24:39,907 --> 01:24:44,287
The perfect weapon for the modern age.
Built for combat.

1088
01:24:44,620 --> 01:24:48,708
With tactical responses more
acute than any human soldier.

1089
01:24:48,791 --> 01:24:50,209
What is that thing?

1090
01:25:06,976 --> 01:25:08,269
They made it.

1091
01:25:09,395 --> 01:25:10,896
Mr. Mills and the other man.

1092
01:25:11,105 --> 01:25:12,398
What man?

1093
01:25:13,649 --> 01:25:14,650
Him.

1094
01:25:14,734 --> 01:25:17,778
Designed
by Mr. Henry Wu

1095
01:25:17,903 --> 01:25:20,448
with an intelligence quotient
comparable to the Velociraptor.

1096
01:25:20,906 --> 01:25:24,201
Biospecs include
a heightened sense of smell

1097
01:25:24,285 --> 01:25:28,873
and trained to respond to a
pulse-coded laser targeting system,

1098
01:25:29,040 --> 01:25:31,876
enabling it to isolate
and track prey

1099
01:25:31,959 --> 01:25:35,087
in complex environments.
Voilà.

1100
01:25:41,802 --> 01:25:44,764
Now, first, the laser
sets the target.

1101
01:25:47,266 --> 01:25:48,726
Once locked on,

1102
01:25:49,435 --> 01:25:51,771
the acoustic signal
triggers the attack.

1103
01:25:58,986 --> 01:26:01,572
This animal is relentless.

1104
01:26:01,822 --> 01:26:05,242
Now, modifications
are still being made...

1105
01:26:05,409 --> 01:26:06,577
20 million!

1106
01:26:10,206 --> 01:26:12,249
No, this is a prototype.

1107
01:26:12,416 --> 01:26:13,626
Uh, not for sale.

1108
01:26:13,959 --> 01:26:14,960
21!

1109
01:26:16,003 --> 01:26:19,382
- It is still a prototype, but...
- 22!

1110
01:26:20,675 --> 01:26:22,343
23!

1111
01:26:23,761 --> 01:26:25,137
24!

1112
01:26:27,306 --> 01:26:29,141
$24 million.

1113
01:26:29,433 --> 01:26:31,185
25 million!

1114
01:26:31,977 --> 01:26:33,187
Do I hear 26?

1115
01:26:33,688 --> 01:26:35,398
That thing
can't leave this building.

1116
01:26:35,731 --> 01:26:36,941
26!

1117
01:26:37,191 --> 01:26:38,734
Any advance on 26?
27!

1118
01:26:38,901 --> 01:26:40,152
$27 million from the back.

1119
01:26:40,319 --> 01:26:42,113
What are you doing?
This animal is not for sale.

1120
01:26:42,279 --> 01:26:44,156
If you wanna create an addict,
you have to give them a taste.

1121
01:26:44,323 --> 01:26:45,366
He's a prototype.

1122
01:26:45,533 --> 01:26:50,454
This prototype is worth
$28 million right now.

1123
01:26:51,247 --> 01:26:53,207
Relax, we'll make some more.

1124
01:26:53,833 --> 01:26:55,126
So will they.

1125
01:27:17,189 --> 01:27:18,566
Hey, buddy.

1126
01:27:19,150 --> 01:27:20,359
You thinking what I'm thinking?

1127
01:27:23,028 --> 01:27:24,447
Once. Twice.

1128
01:27:25,114 --> 01:27:27,616
Sold to the Russian friend.

1129
01:27:28,868 --> 01:27:31,495
Well done, sir.
And congratulations...

1130
01:27:31,662 --> 01:27:33,748
...for
this magnificent animal.

1131
01:28:30,638 --> 01:28:31,639
Oh, God!

1132
01:28:43,150 --> 01:28:44,151
Get this thing out of here!

1133
01:28:44,819 --> 01:28:46,028
Hey, you! Turn it out!

1134
01:29:36,996 --> 01:29:38,247
Mills!

1135
01:29:38,998 --> 01:29:40,624
Mills, where are you?

1136
01:29:41,542 --> 01:29:43,168
I want my bonus!

1137
01:29:49,967 --> 01:29:51,385
Holy cow!

1138
01:29:51,927 --> 01:29:53,304
What are you?

1139
01:29:54,305 --> 01:29:56,181
I didn't see you on the island.

1140
01:30:14,408 --> 01:30:15,784
Oh, you're a tough guy.

1141
01:30:49,318 --> 01:30:51,028
Look at you.

1142
01:30:52,863 --> 01:30:56,951
You're some kind of hot rod
with really pretty teeth.

1143
01:30:58,619 --> 01:31:01,747
This'll make a perfect
centerpiece for my necklace.

1144
01:31:20,891 --> 01:31:22,393
Open wide.

1145
01:31:46,959 --> 01:31:48,877
No. No.

1146
01:31:50,629 --> 01:31:51,964
Please.

1147
01:32:14,445 --> 01:32:15,487
Shh!

1148
01:32:16,488 --> 01:32:18,407
Move! Move!
Out of the way!

1149
01:33:03,827 --> 01:33:04,912
Don't!

1150
01:33:08,457 --> 01:33:10,334
You two, you deserve each other.

1151
01:33:11,126 --> 01:33:12,127
Maisie, come with me.

1152
01:33:12,836 --> 01:33:15,130
You got your money,
now walk away.

1153
01:33:15,506 --> 01:33:16,673
Oh, what are you gonna do, huh?

1154
01:33:16,757 --> 01:33:19,301
We're gonna stop this.
All of it.

1155
01:33:19,593 --> 01:33:20,803
How?

1156
01:33:20,969 --> 01:33:23,305
What, are you gonna go back in time
before Hammond decided to play God?

1157
01:33:23,472 --> 01:33:24,515
You can't put it back
in the box!

1158
01:33:24,848 --> 01:33:26,683
We have to try.
It's too late.

1159
01:33:26,767 --> 01:33:28,143
Maisie, come.

1160
01:33:30,562 --> 01:33:34,191
Oh! So, you're gonna
take care of her now?

1161
01:33:34,775 --> 01:33:35,776
Huh?

1162
01:33:36,360 --> 01:33:38,320
You have no idea what she is.

1163
01:33:39,488 --> 01:33:42,241
What do you think drove Hammond
and Lockwood apart, huh?

1164
01:33:42,908 --> 01:33:46,328
Lockwood never had a grandchild.

1165
01:33:47,079 --> 01:33:48,997
He just wanted
his daughter back.

1166
01:33:51,750 --> 01:33:53,293
And he had the technology.

1167
01:33:54,586 --> 01:33:56,421
He created another.

1168
01:33:58,048 --> 01:33:59,550
He made her again.

1169
01:34:03,470 --> 01:34:04,471
Go.

1170
01:34:10,060 --> 01:34:12,146
Come on!

1171
01:34:13,105 --> 01:34:14,398
Come on.

1172
01:34:15,274 --> 01:34:17,526
Eggs need to go
in a sterile environment.

1173
01:34:19,236 --> 01:34:20,737
Where are those genome kits?

1174
01:34:27,494 --> 01:34:30,581
Get this to Mills. Be very
careful, it's extremely valuable.

1175
01:34:30,664 --> 01:34:32,583
You, I need blood samples
from the raptor.

1176
01:34:33,834 --> 01:34:35,252
Don't just stand there
like an idiot!

1177
01:34:35,335 --> 01:34:38,005
Get me 50 ccs carfentanil
and a phlebotomy kit!

1178
01:34:38,088 --> 01:34:39,840
It's right there! Now!

1179
01:34:40,799 --> 01:34:42,384
Come on. Come on.

1180
01:34:43,468 --> 01:34:44,678
I need this raptor's blood.

1181
01:34:46,680 --> 01:34:48,307
Sure, go ahead.

1182
01:34:48,807 --> 01:34:50,559
Where is that kit?

1183
01:34:51,101 --> 01:34:52,603
Okay, now listen to me.

1184
01:34:52,895 --> 01:34:54,313
It is better for you
to cooperate.

1185
01:34:54,396 --> 01:34:55,397
I'm gonna get this blood

1186
01:34:55,731 --> 01:34:56,982
with or without your help.

1187
01:34:57,149 --> 01:34:58,483
This blood's contaminated.

1188
01:34:58,650 --> 01:35:01,195
I designed this animal myself.

1189
01:35:01,278 --> 01:35:02,279
It's pure.

1190
01:35:02,738 --> 01:35:06,158
Every cell of her body was created
in the most controlled environment

1191
01:35:06,283 --> 01:35:08,410
under the most demanding
of conditions.

1192
01:35:08,911 --> 01:35:11,872
Yeah, but I did an intravenous
blood transfusion with a T. rex,

1193
01:35:11,955 --> 01:35:13,624
so, it's a sock drawer in there.

1194
01:35:13,999 --> 01:35:15,334
You what?

1195
01:35:16,627 --> 01:35:18,295
Franklin!
What are you doing?

1196
01:35:19,463 --> 01:35:21,006
Saving your life!

1197
01:35:25,135 --> 01:35:26,803
Yes!

1198
01:35:27,054 --> 01:35:28,263
Stop!

1199
01:35:30,098 --> 01:35:31,934
Don't move.

1200
01:35:36,605 --> 01:35:39,358
Now step away
from that cage. Now.

1201
01:35:48,116 --> 01:35:49,868
Easy now.

1202
01:36:16,019 --> 01:36:17,604
Run. Run!

1203
01:38:19,810 --> 01:38:21,478
Dave, do you copy?
Dave, you there?

1204
01:38:21,812 --> 01:38:22,854
Run!

1205
01:39:47,772 --> 01:39:48,773
You see that?

1206
01:39:49,566 --> 01:39:51,026
That's hydrogen cyanide.

1207
01:39:51,193 --> 01:39:53,778
If it reaches the containment
level, it'll kill them all.

1208
01:39:55,655 --> 01:39:57,282
The ventilation system is down.

1209
01:39:57,407 --> 01:39:58,533
Can you bring it back up?

1210
01:40:31,441 --> 01:40:33,693
The server's unresponsive.

1211
01:40:33,777 --> 01:40:34,986
I have to reboot
the whole system.

1212
01:40:35,070 --> 01:40:36,071
So, reboot it.
I'm trying.

1213
01:40:53,964 --> 01:40:55,048
What?

1214
01:40:57,133 --> 01:40:59,052
Franklin, we don't have time.
I know, I know, I know.

1215
01:41:00,470 --> 01:41:01,638
Okay.

1216
01:41:41,219 --> 01:41:42,345
Go. Go.

1217
01:41:42,512 --> 01:41:44,556
Run, run.

1218
01:42:39,319 --> 01:42:41,112
Hey.
Here. You're okay.

1219
01:42:41,529 --> 01:42:42,864
Put pressure on it.
Don't look at it.

1220
01:42:43,073 --> 01:42:44,324
Look at me.
Look at me.

1221
01:42:44,491 --> 01:42:45,617
You have to go find her.

1222
01:42:45,742 --> 01:42:46,826
I can't leave you here.

1223
01:42:50,830 --> 01:42:53,375
Go. Go, I'll be fine.

1224
01:42:53,458 --> 01:42:54,459
Run!

1225
01:45:23,733 --> 01:45:25,276
Maisie, stay down!

1226
01:46:10,113 --> 01:46:11,322
Shh!

1227
01:46:30,174 --> 01:46:32,969
Follow me. I know
a way on the other side.

1228
01:46:36,097 --> 01:46:37,098
Come on.

1229
01:46:40,226 --> 01:46:41,477
Come on!

1230
01:46:58,244 --> 01:46:59,621
This way!

1231
01:47:32,570 --> 01:47:33,571
I got you.

1232
01:47:46,918 --> 01:47:47,919
Hey!

1233
01:48:17,407 --> 01:48:18,616
Come on.

1234
01:49:43,242 --> 01:49:44,660
What the hell?

1235
01:49:47,455 --> 01:49:48,539
Hey!

1236
01:49:50,833 --> 01:49:51,959
Hey!

1237
01:49:52,126 --> 01:49:53,127
You good?

1238
01:49:55,546 --> 01:49:56,881
Yeah. You?

1239
01:49:57,131 --> 01:49:58,132
Nope.

1240
01:49:58,633 --> 01:50:00,301
Hey, we got a problem
downstairs.

1241
01:50:00,384 --> 01:50:01,469
I need you to come see this.

1242
01:50:31,916 --> 01:50:33,251
They're all dying.

1243
01:50:33,417 --> 01:50:35,670
The blast damaged
the ventilation system.

1244
01:50:36,003 --> 01:50:37,880
We did everything we could.

1245
01:50:41,259 --> 01:50:42,677
I can open the gates from here.

1246
01:50:44,053 --> 01:50:45,680
Claire, be careful.

1247
01:50:47,348 --> 01:50:49,225
We're not on an island anymore.

1248
01:51:44,947 --> 01:51:45,990
Claire.

1249
01:51:46,407 --> 01:51:48,993
You press that button,
there is no going back.

1250
01:51:54,582 --> 01:51:56,083
We can't let them die.

1251
01:52:46,384 --> 01:52:47,927
I had to.

1252
01:52:49,053 --> 01:52:50,554
They're alive,

1253
01:52:51,055 --> 01:52:52,556
like me.

1254
01:53:24,547 --> 01:53:27,550
Don't! Don't!

1255
01:55:05,856 --> 01:55:07,191
Hey, girl.

1256
01:55:07,274 --> 01:55:08,275
Owen.

1257
01:55:08,359 --> 01:55:09,610
Shh!

1258
01:55:10,736 --> 01:55:12,405
It's okay, she won't hurt us.

1259
01:55:34,969 --> 01:55:35,970
Hey.

1260
01:55:44,437 --> 01:55:46,105
Blue, come with me.

1261
01:55:51,819 --> 01:55:53,988
We'll take you
to a safe place, okay?

1262
01:56:35,696 --> 01:56:37,615
How many times do you
have to see the evidence?

1263
01:56:38,616 --> 01:56:40,743
How many times
must the point be made?

1264
01:56:42,453 --> 01:56:44,288
We're causing
our own extinction.

1265
01:56:45,706 --> 01:56:47,249
Too many red lines
have been crossed.

1266
01:56:49,043 --> 01:56:52,713
And our home has, in fundamental
ways, been polluted

1267
01:56:52,922 --> 01:56:57,301
by avarice
and political megalomania.

1268
01:56:58,427 --> 01:57:00,763
Genetic power has
now been unleashed

1269
01:57:01,305 --> 01:57:03,557
and of course,
that's gonna be catastrophic.

1270
01:57:04,308 --> 01:57:05,518
This change was inevitable

1271
01:57:05,601 --> 01:57:08,187
from the moment we brought the first
dinosaur back from extinction.

1272
01:57:10,022 --> 01:57:12,066
We convince ourselves
that sudden change

1273
01:57:12,149 --> 01:57:14,068
is something that happens outside
the normal order of things,

1274
01:57:14,193 --> 01:57:16,278
like a car crash,

1275
01:57:17,029 --> 01:57:19,448
or that it's beyond our
control, like a fatal illness.

1276
01:57:19,532 --> 01:57:23,285
We don't conceive of sudden,
radical, irrational change

1277
01:57:23,369 --> 01:57:26,205
as woven into
the very fabric of existence.

1278
01:57:27,373 --> 01:57:30,376
Yet, I can assure you,
it most assuredly is.

1279
01:57:30,501 --> 01:57:32,169
And it's happening now.

1280
01:57:35,381 --> 01:57:38,884
Humans and dinosaurs are now
gonna be forced to coexist.

1281
01:57:42,972 --> 01:57:45,516
These creatures
were here before us.

1282
01:57:46,684 --> 01:57:48,852
And if we're not careful,
they're gonna be here after.

1283
01:57:55,276 --> 01:57:58,279
We're gonna have to adjust to new
threats that we can't imagine.

1284
01:58:05,035 --> 01:58:06,662
We've entered a new era.

1285
01:58:08,706 --> 01:58:11,208
Welcome to Jurassic World.

